\section{Resolving the source}
\label{sec:resolve-source}

The [[play]], [[list]], and [[analyse]] commands accept an
optional source that can be a directory (containing
[[.learnlog/]]), a bundle file, or a ProgSnap2 zip.
We need a single function that resolves any of these into a
[[LearnlogRepo]] ready for querying.

For bundle files, we clone them into a temporary directory.
For ProgSnap2 files, we reconstruct a temporary Git repository
from the exported code states and run events.
The caller is responsible for cleanup---we return both the repo
and the temporary directory (or [[None]] if no temp dir was created).

We detect ProgSnap2 sources with [[zipfile.is_zipfile]], then fall
back to asking [[git bundle list-heads]] to parse the file rather
than relying on file extensions.
This stays robust even when the user renames a bundle to something
like [[student-log.bin]], and it avoids [[git bundle verify]]'s
requirement that the current working directory is itself a Git
repository.

<<helper functions>>=
def resolve_source(source=None):
    """Resolve a source into a ``(LearnlogRepo, tmpdir)`` pair.

    ``source`` can be ``None`` (search current directory),
    a directory path, a ProgSnap2 zip file, or a bundle file.
    When a temporary repository is used, ``tmpdir`` is the
    temporary directory that should be cleaned up by the caller;
    otherwise it is ``None``.
    """
    if source is None:
        workdir = find_learnlog_dir()
        if workdir is None:
            raise SystemExit(
                "No .learnlog/ directory found. "
                "Run this from a learnlog-tracked project."
            )
        return LearnlogRepo(workdir), None

    path = pathlib.Path(source).resolve()

    if path.is_dir():
        if not (path / ".learnlog").is_dir():
            raise SystemExit(
                f"No .learnlog/ directory in {path}"
            )
        return LearnlogRepo(path), None

    if zipfile.is_zipfile(path):
        return read_progsnap2_as_repo(path)

    <<resolve bundle file>>
@

To unbundle, we create a temporary directory and use
[[git clone]] with the bundle as the remote.
A temporary directory is required because cloning directly into the
caller's working tree would write a [[remote.origin.url]] pointing at
the bundle path into [[.learnlog/config]], leaving the user with a
remote they did not configure.
We point [[GIT_DIR]] at the cloned repo's [[.git/]] so that
[[LearnlogRepo.git()]] can query it.
However, [[LearnlogRepo]] expects the git directory to be named
[[.learnlog/]], so we rename it after cloning.
Both Git calls go through [[run_git]] (\cref{sec:starting-git})
rather than the wrapper, because there is no repository yet to build
a wrapper on; a machine without Git is then reported the same way as
everywhere else.

<<resolve bundle file>>=
if not path.is_file():
    raise SystemExit(f"Not a file or directory: {path}")

verify = run_git(
    ["bundle", "list-heads", str(path)],
    capture_output=True, text=True, encoding="utf-8", errors="replace",
)
if verify.returncode != 0:
    raise SystemExit(
        f"Not a valid bundle file: {path}"
    )

tmpdir = tempfile.mkdtemp(prefix="learnlog-play-")
try:
    <<clone the bundle into the temporary directory>>
except BaseException:
    shutil.rmtree(tmpdir, ignore_errors=True)
    raise
@

Ownership of that directory changes hands halfway through the branch,
and the guard exists to make the handover total.
Once we return, the directory is the caller's---the return value says
so---and the caller frees it in its own [[finally]].
Until then nobody else knows the name, so if we leave by any other
route than [[return]], the directory becomes unreachable garbage in
[[/tmp]] that no later run will recognise or collect.
Cloning a bundle is slow enough for the student to change their mind
about, and [[Ctrl-C]] is how they say so; the clone can also fail
on a bundle Git could list but not resolve.

\paragraph{Why the broad net.}

[[except BaseException]] is normally a warning sign, and deserves the
argument for why it is the right net here rather than the usual
[[except Exception]].
The three things it catches beyond [[except Exception]]---
[[KeyboardInterrupt]], [[SystemExit]], [[GeneratorExit]]---are exactly
the ones a program must not quietly absorb, which is what makes the
broad net a bad habit in general.
What disarms the objection is the bare [[raise]]: the handler decides
nothing about the exception, it frees a resource on the way past and
lets the exception continue to whoever it was for.
Catching less would not be safer, only leakier.
And catching less would miss the case that actually happens:
[[Ctrl-C]] is the one exit from here a person takes deliberately, and
it is precisely the one [[except Exception]] is defined not to see.

<<clone the bundle into the temporary directory>>=
tmppath = pathlib.Path(tmpdir)

run_git(
    ["clone", str(path), str(tmppath / "repo")],
    capture_output=True, text=True, encoding="utf-8", errors="replace", check=True,
)

cloned = tmppath / "repo"
(cloned / ".git").rename(cloned / ".learnlog")

return LearnlogRepo(cloned), tmpdir
