\subsection{Exports appear all at once}
\label{sec:staged-export}

Where the export lands is ordinarily the work tree, because that is
where the student is standing when they run the command.
That makes the file two things at once: an output, and part of the
directory the \emph{next} run snapshots.
The exclude patterns settle whether it is committed
(\cref{sec:ensure-configured}); this settles what a reader can see
while it is being written.

A bundle of a term's log takes long enough to write that a run
starting in the middle of it is not a thought experiment, and a run
that snapshots a half-written file records a file that never existed.
Writing beside the destination and renaming solves it the way it is
always solved: [[os.replace]] within a directory is atomic, so the
name either does not exist or names a finished export.

The staging file takes the destination's extension rather than a
neutral one, which is what puts it under the same exclude pattern as
the result.
A [[.tmp]] file would be excluded by nothing, so the very run this is
protecting against would commit the half-written bundle under its
temporary name instead.

The two exporters differ only in what they write, so the staging is
written once and takes the writer as an argument.

<<export functions>>=
def write_export_atomically(export, repo, output):
    """Write an export through a file beside ``output``.

    Calls ``export(repo, path)`` on a temporary file in the
    destination's directory and moves it onto ``output``, so that
    the destination never names a partial export.
    """
    try:
        handle, name = tempfile.mkstemp(
            dir=output.parent,
            prefix=EXPORT_STAGING_PREFIX,
            suffix=output.suffix,
        )
    except OSError as error:
        raise SystemExit(f"Cannot write to {output}: {error}")
    os.close(handle)
    staged = pathlib.Path(name)
    try:
        export(repo, staged)
    except BaseException:
        staged.unlink(missing_ok=True)
        raise
    os.replace(staged, output)
<<constants>>=
EXPORT_STAGING_PREFIX = ".learnlog-export-"
@

Cleaning up on [[BaseException]] rather than [[Exception]] is what
covers the interruption this is about: a student who gives up on a
slow export with [[Ctrl-C]] should be left with the export they had
before, not with a staging file the next run has to be told to ignore.
Re-raising keeps the interruption an interruption.

Let's verify that the destination is written by a rename and not by
the exporter itself:

<<test functions>>=
def test_export_goes_through_a_staging_file(
    workdir, monkeypatch,
):
    """The destination appears complete or not at all."""
    from learnlog.cli import app
    from learnlog import cli
    from typer.testing import CliRunner
    LearnlogRepo(workdir)
    renames = []
    real_replace = cli.os.replace

    def record(source, destination):
        renames.append((source, destination))
        return real_replace(source, destination)

    monkeypatch.setattr(cli.os, "replace", record)
    monkeypatch.chdir(workdir)
    bundle = workdir / "out.learnlog"
    result = CliRunner().invoke(
        app, ["export", "-o", str(bundle)]
    )
    assert result.exit_code == 0
    staged = [
        source for source, destination in renames
        if str(destination) == str(bundle)
    ]
    assert len(staged) == 1
    assert str(staged[0]) != str(bundle)
    assert pathlib.Path(staged[0]).suffix == ".learnlog"
    assert bundle.exists()

def test_export_names_match_the_excluded_patterns(workdir):
    """The exclude patterns must cover what export writes."""
    from learnlog.cli import (
        DEFAULT_BUNDLE_NAME, DEFAULT_PROGSNAP2_NAME,
    )
    from learnlog.gitrepo import (
        BUNDLE_SUFFIX, PROGSNAP2_SUFFIX,
    )
    assert DEFAULT_BUNDLE_NAME.endswith(BUNDLE_SUFFIX)
    assert DEFAULT_PROGSNAP2_NAME.endswith(PROGSNAP2_SUFFIX)
