\subsection{The Python setup pipeline}
\label{sec:setup-pipeline}

[[init_python]] chains the helpers together.  There are
three legitimate end states: install into an
already-activated venv (the VS Code scenario, discussed
next), install into a pre-existing [[workdir/.venv/]]
(idempotent re-run of [[init]]), or create a fresh
[[workdir/.venv/]] using [[virtualenv]] and install
there.  In every case, the final step is
[[write_autostart_files]].

\paragraph{Honouring an active venv.}
A very common way students meet [[init]] is through VS
Code: opening the workspace auto-activates a venv inside
the integrated terminal, the student runs
[[pipx install learnlog]] or, on machines still stuck on
system Python~3.9, [[uv tool install --python 3.10
learnlog]], and then [[learnlog init python]].  The
student's expectation is \enquote{install
into the environment I have active}---not
\enquote{silently create a second venv alongside it}.
All venv activation scripts (POSIX, fish, PowerShell,
Poetry, Hatch, and VS Code's Python extension) set the
[[VIRTUAL_ENV]] environment variable to the venv root.
When it is set and [[--python]] was not given, we treat
that venv as the install target.  [[--python]] still wins
when explicitly passed, because someone who typed it knows
what they want.

\paragraph{Idempotency.}
Students will occasionally re-run [[init]]---after moving
machines, after nuking a venv, or out of superstition.
Filesystem presence of [[workdir/.venv/]] is the
idempotency signal (rather than a sentinel we write), so
manually-created venvs are also respected.  [[pip
install]] itself is a no-op when the package is already
up to date, so re-running the full pipeline is cheap.

\paragraph{Why the \texttt{virtualenv} Python API.}
We invoke [[virtualenv.cli_run(["--python", target, dest])]]
rather than [[subprocess.run([sys.executable, "-m",
"virtualenv", ...])]].  The subprocess form would pin the
new venv's plumbing to the interpreter that ran the CLI
command, which under [[pipx]] is pipx's private Python
inside [[~/.local/share/pipx/venvs/learnlog/]]---the exact
trap discussed in \cref{sec:setup-target-python}.  The
library form sidesteps [[sys.executable]] entirely and
lets [[--python]] dictate the target.  Importing
[[virtualenv]] lazily (only on this branch, inside the
function) also keeps [[learnlog]]'s other CLI commands
snappy, since nothing else needs it at import time.

\paragraph{Why \texttt{python -m pip}.}
The install goes through the venv's interpreter,
[[python -m pip install learnlog]], not through the [[pip]]
launcher next to it.  That is the form pip's own
documentation recommends, and it removes a moving part: on
Windows the launcher is a small [[pip.exe]] that a failed
self-upgrade leaves broken, whereas the venv's
[[python.exe]] is what ran [[learnlog]] itself and is
therefore known to work.  The venv that made this matter is
the one from \cref{sec:setup-pip-in-venv}, populated from
both PowerShell and WSL; [[find_in_venv]] now picks the
interpreter the host can run, and going through it keeps
[[init]] out of the launcher business altogether.

\paragraph{Why the install has its own function.}
The student who reported the WinError got it as a full
traceback: Typer's rich rendering of every frame from
[[init]] down to [[CreateProcess]], with the one line that
mattered at the very bottom.  The audience is beginners in
an editor's terminal, who mail the whole wall to the
teacher instead of acting on it.  [[find_in_venv]] already
turns its failure into a one-line [[SystemExit]], and the
app does the same for a missing Git, so the install follows
suit.  Two failures are told apart because they call for
different actions: an [[OSError]] means the interpreter
itself could not be started, which is a broken venv;
[[CalledProcessError]] means pip ran and refused, typically
without network, and pip's own output, which keeps the
inherited terminal so the student sees it, already says
why---our sentence only points at it.

<<helper functions>>=
def install_learnlog(venv_dir):
    """Install learnlog into ``venv_dir`` with its own interpreter.

    Runs ``python -m pip install learnlog``.  A venv whose
    interpreter cannot be started, or a pip that fails, ends in
    ``SystemExit`` with one sentence rather than a traceback.
    """
    python = find_in_venv(venv_dir, "python")
    try:
        subprocess.run(
            [str(python), "-m", "pip", "install", "learnlog"],
            check=True,
        )
    except OSError as error:
        raise SystemExit(
            f"Could not run {python}: {error}"
        ) from error
    except subprocess.CalledProcessError as error:
        raise SystemExit(
            f"pip install learnlog failed in {venv_dir} "
            f"(exit status {error.returncode}); "
            "see pip's message above."
        ) from error
@

