\section{Diagnosing the autostart wiring}
\label{sec:autostart-diagnosis}

Autostart is the one part of learnlog that is invisible while it
works, which is precisely what makes it hard to notice when it
stops.  A venv whose marker names a project the student has since
deleted, renamed, or unmounted resolves to nothing, and the run
path answers that by declining to log rather than by inventing a
repository beside whatever the student happens to be working on
(\cref{sec:autostart-wired}).  Declining is the right call---the
alternative is an unbounded repository in the wrong place---but it
is silent by construction: a student's program must behave
identically with and without learnlog, so the refusal cannot print
a word.  Weeks of runs can go missing from a dataset with nothing
anywhere to say so.

The CLI carries no such obligation.  Nothing a student runs
depends on what [[learnlog doctor]] prints, so this is the one
place that can afford to say out loud what the runtime decided in
silence.

Three states are worth telling apart, because each calls for a
different response:
\begin{description}
\item[Not wired] No marker file sits next to this interpreter's
    [[site-packages]].  Nothing is broken---the interpreter was
    simply never prepared by [[learnlog init]], which is also the
    normal state for a student who writes an explicit
    \mintinline{python}{import learnlog} line.  The remedy, if one
    is wanted at all, is [[init]].
\item[Wired and anchored] The marker, or the [[sys.prefix]]
    backup, resolves to a project that still holds a
    [[.learnlog/]].  Runs are being recorded, and the report says
    into which project---which doubles as the answer to
    \enquote{why are my runs landing in the wrong log?}
\item[Wired but dead] A marker exists, so this interpreter was
    told which project it serves, yet neither the marker nor the
    backup resolves.  This is the silent-data-loss state, and the
    only one [[doctor]] can repair.
\end{description}

Notice what separates the first state from the third: both resolve
to no project at all, and only the \emph{presence} of the marker
file tells them apart.  That is the same distinction
[[initialize()]] draws before refusing to log
(\cref{sec:autostart-wired}), which is why [[doctor]] asks
[[_is_autostart_wired]] instead of inspecting the [[.pth]] file
itself: a diagnosis that used a different test from the runtime's
would eventually disagree with it, and a diagnosis that disagrees
with the thing it diagnoses is worse than none.

<<constants>>=
class AutostartState(enum.Enum):
    """How the running interpreter is wired for autostart.

    ``unwired`` means no marker file exists, ``anchored`` that
    the wiring resolves to a project, and ``dead`` that a marker
    exists but resolves to nothing -- the state in which runs go
    unrecorded with no sign of it.
    """

    unwired = "unwired"
    anchored = "anchored"
    dead = "dead"
@

For the dead state, the single most useful fact is the path the
marker actually holds: [[/home/alice/lab1]] tells a student who
renamed a directory exactly what happened, whereas \enquote{the
project could not be resolved} tells them nothing they can act
on.  We show the raw recorded text rather than anything derived
from it, because the discrepancy between what is recorded and what
exists is the whole finding.  A marker that cannot be read at all
is a different fault with the same consequence, so it reports
itself in the same slot instead of raising: a diagnostic command
that crashes on the very file it was asked to explain would leave
the student worse off than the silence they came to ask about.
That is also why the [[except]] clause is broad---the marker is
arbitrary bytes on a disk we do not control, and a permission
error and a half-written file are the same news here.

<<helper functions>>=
def marker_contents(marker_path):
    """Return the marker file's text, or why it cannot be read."""
    try:
        return pathlib.Path(marker_path).read_text(encoding="utf-8", errors="replace").strip()
    except Exception as exc:
        return f"<unreadable: {exc}>"
@

The report is a single function so that the command itself has
nothing left to decide.  It takes the same two overrides as the
resolver it calls, [[marker_path]] and [[sys_prefix]], and
defaults both to the running interpreter's values.  Mirroring
[[_resolve_project_root]]'s signature here is deliberate: the
defaults keep the command a no-argument call, while the parameters
let the tests drive every branch against a scratch directory
instead of building a real venv.

Resolving has one side effect worth naming.  When the marker is
stale but the [[sys.prefix]] backup still finds the project,
[[_resolve_project_root]] rewrites the marker (\cref{learnlog}).
A moved project is therefore repaired by the mere act of asking,
and---more to the point---the report describes what the next run
will actually do rather than what the file happened to say a
moment earlier.

<<helper functions>>=
def autostart_report(marker_path=None, sys_prefix=None):
    """Describe how the running interpreter is wired to log.

    Returns a ``(state, lines)`` pair, where ``state`` is an
    ``AutostartState`` and ``lines`` are ready to print.  Both
    arguments default to the running interpreter's own marker
    path and ``sys.prefix``.
    """
    <<default the report inputs to this interpreter>>
    <<report an interpreter nobody wired>>
    <<report the project the wiring resolves to>>
    <<report wiring that resolves to nothing>>
@

Every report opens with the interpreter it is about.  That line is
not decoration: a student who installed learnlog with [[pipx]], or
who forgot to activate the project venv, is asking about a
different environment from the one their programs run in, and
\enquote{not wired} only becomes intelligible next to the name of
what was inspected.

<<default the report inputs to this interpreter>>=
if marker_path is None:
    marker_path = _DEFAULT_MARKER_PATH
if sys_prefix is None:
    sys_prefix = sys.prefix
marker_path = pathlib.Path(marker_path)
lines = [f"Interpreter: {sys_prefix}"]
@

An unwired interpreter still gets a [[Marker]] line, naming the
directory the marker would have lived in.  It is what turns a bare
\enquote{not wired} into something checkable: the student who
expected this environment to be wired can compare that directory
against the venv they actually ran [[init]] in.

<<report an interpreter nobody wired>>=
if not _is_autostart_wired(marker_path=marker_path):
    return AutostartState.unwired, lines + [
        "Autostart:   not wired -- runs of this interpreter "
        "are not recorded.",
        f"Marker:      no {marker_path.name} in "
        f"{marker_path.parent}",
        "Run 'learnlog init python' in your project to wire "
        "this environment.",
    ]
@

Past that point the interpreter is wired, and the only remaining
question is whether the wiring still lands anywhere.  We answer it
with [[_resolve_project_root]] rather than by reading the marker
ourselves, so that both of the runtime's sources count: an
anchored report covers the project named by the marker \emph{and}
the one recovered from [[sys.prefix]] when the marker has gone
stale.  Reporting only the first would call a self-healing venv
broken.

<<report the project the wiring resolves to>>=
project_root = _resolve_project_root(
    marker_path=marker_path, sys_prefix=sys_prefix,
)
if project_root is not None:
    return AutostartState.anchored, lines + [
        "Autostart:   wired",
        f"Project:     {project_root}",
        f"Runs of this interpreter are recorded in "
        f"{pathlib.Path(project_root) / '.learnlog'}.",
    ]
@

What falls through is the state the command exists for: a marker
is present, so somebody ran [[init]] for this interpreter, and
neither source resolves, so nothing is being recorded.  Both facts
are already established by the time we get here, which is why this
last block asserts nothing and simply says what it knows.

<<report wiring that resolves to nothing>>=
return AutostartState.dead, lines + [
    "Autostart:   wired, but the project it names is gone -- "
    "runs are NOT being recorded.",
    f"Marker:      {marker_path} names "
    f"{marker_contents(marker_path)}",
    "Run 'learnlog doctor --repair' from your project "
    "directory to re-wire this interpreter.",
]
@

Let's verify the classification.  A scratch directory stands in
for [[site-packages]], and the presence and contents of the marker
file inside it select the state, exactly as they would in a real
venv:

<<test functions>>=
def test_autostart_report_unwired_without_marker(tmp_path):
    """No marker file means nobody wired this interpreter."""
    from learnlog.cli import AutostartState, autostart_report
    purelib = tmp_path / "site-packages"
    purelib.mkdir()
