\section{Typer application}

We define the Typer app at module level with sixteen
commands and one command group, [[metrics]].
The [[app]] object is the entry point referenced by
[[pyproject.toml]].
It is built on a group class of our own, which exists for one
failure every command shares and none should have to handle itself.

\subsection{A missing Git is a sentence, not a traceback}
\label{sec:learnlog-group}

Nearly every command begins by constructing a [[LearnlogRepo]], and
the constructor runs Git (\cref{sec:init-repo}).
On a machine without Git that raises [[GitNotFoundError]]
(\cref{sec:starting-git}) before the command has done anything, and
nothing in the command catches it, so Typer prints a traceback.
The traceback ends in the right sentence, but a student who has not
installed Git is the reader least equipped to find it there.

The group is the one place every command passes through.
Typer runs a subcommand inside its group's [[invoke]], including the
commands of the [[metrics]] and [[tutorial]] sub-groups, so one
override covers them all, where a [[try]] in each command would be
forgotten at the next command added.
A callback cannot do this, because it runs before the subcommand
rather than around it.
Nor can the console script: [[pyproject.toml]] points at [[app]] and
so do the tests, through [[CliRunner]], so a wrapper function would
be exercised by neither.

Only [[GitNotFoundError]] is converted.
A [[GitError]] with an exit status means Git ran and refused, which
is a damaged log or a bug in learnlog; both deserve the traceback,
or the more specific sentence that the commands expecting such a
refusal already produce (\cref{sec:export,sec:checkout}).
[[SystemExit]] with a string is how every other refusal in this
module is delivered---exit status 1, the message on standard
error---and, unlike [[typer.Exit]], it carries the message, which
here is the whole point.

<<constants>>=
class LearnlogGroup(TyperGroup):
    """The learnlog command group.

    Adds the one handler every subcommand would otherwise need: a
    Git that cannot be found is reported as a sentence naming the
    fix, not as a traceback.
    """

    def invoke(self, ctx):
        try:
            return super().invoke(ctx)
        except GitNotFoundError as error:
            raise SystemExit(str(error)) from error
@

The repository is built before [[PATH]] is scrubbed, because building
it runs Git; the scrub reaches the command because [[git_environment]]
copies the process environment (\cref{sec:git-environment}).
The [[SystemExit]] assertion is the one that matters: [[CliRunner]]
reports exit status 1 for any uncaught exception, so without it the
test would pass on the very traceback it exists to forbid.

<<test functions>>=
def test_a_missing_git_is_a_sentence_not_a_traceback(
    workdir, monkeypatch,
):
    """A student without Git gets the fix, not a stack trace."""
    from learnlog.cli import app
    from typer.testing import CliRunner
    LearnlogRepo(workdir)
    monkeypatch.chdir(workdir)
    monkeypatch.setenv("PATH", str(workdir / "no-such-bin"))
    result = CliRunner().invoke(app, ["list"])
    assert result.exit_code == 1
    assert isinstance(result.exception, SystemExit)
    assert "Install Git" in result.output
@

