\subsection{Running the CLI as a module}
\label{sec:module-entry}

[[python -m learnlog]] is the spelling that works when the venv's
[[Scripts/]] or [[bin/]] is not on [[PATH]]---an unactivated Windows
venv, typically---and the one students already know from
[[python -m pip]].
Without an entry point neither spelling worked:
[[python -m learnlog]] stopped with \enquote{No module named
learnlog.\_\_main\_\_}, and [[python -m learnlog.cli]] exited with
status 0 having done nothing.
Either way the autostart hook had already written the run's header,
so the failure was recorded as one of the student's own runs.
The recorder now recognises both spellings as the CLI
(\cref{sec:veto}), so the veto and the review classification hold
whichever way the command is typed, and the package gets the
[[__main__.py]] that makes the first spelling run.

Passing [[prog_name]] keeps the usage text saying [[learnlog]]
however the command was started: Click would otherwise print
[[python -m learnlog]] or [[python -m learnlog.cli]], and the help a
student reads should name the command the tutorials teach.

<<[[__main__.py]]>>=
"""Run the learnlog CLI as `python -m learnlog`."""
from learnlog.cli import PROG_NAME, app

app(prog_name=PROG_NAME)
<<constants>>=
PROG_NAME = "learnlog"
@

[[python -m learnlog.cli]] needs one more step.
[[runpy]] runs this file under the name [[__main__]], which is a
second copy of the module beside the [[learnlog.cli]] that the rest of
the package imports (the ProgSnap2 exporter imports it lazily, for
one).
Running the canonical module's [[app]] keeps a single set of
definitions in play.

<<typer app>>=
if __name__ == "__main__":
    from learnlog.cli import app as canonical_app

    canonical_app(prog_name=PROG_NAME)
@

Both spellings are checked in a subprocess, since running a module is
exactly what [[CliRunner]] skips.

<<test functions>>=
@pytest.mark.parametrize("module", ["learnlog", "learnlog.cli"])
def test_the_cli_runs_as_a_module(module):
    result = subprocess.run(
        [sys.executable, "-m", module, "--help"],
        capture_output=True, text=True, timeout=60,
        env=dict(os.environ, LEARNLOG_SKIP_AUTOSTART="1"),
    )
    assert result.returncode == 0
    assert "Usage: learnlog" in result.stdout
