\subsection{The [[tutorial]] command}
\label{sec:tutorial}

The tutorial subcommand delegates to [[pytorial]] rather than
reimplementing a second step engine inside [[learnlog]].
That keeps the host CLI focused on learnlog's own behavior while
still letting us ship guided walkthroughs for common tasks such as
project setup, playback, sharing, and ProgSnap2 analysis.

We mount pytorial's embedded command tree under [[learnlog
tutorial]], which gives us [[list]], [[run]], and [[review]]
without the standalone [[install]] command that only makes sense
for pytorial's top-level CLI.
Passing [[namespace="learnlog"]] keeps pytorial's persisted state
isolated under a learnlog-specific directory instead of mixing it
with other embedded hosts on the same machine.
The builtin [[using-tutorials]] self-tour is prepended
automatically, and the rest of the catalog comes from the Markdown
tutorials we ship as package data under [[learnlog/tutorials/]].
We list those tutorial files explicitly so [[learnlog tutorial
list]] follows the intended student-to-researcher progression
instead of an incidental alphabetical order.
The first three tutorials come in a novice and an expert variant
tangled from the same literate source; each expert twin is listed
directly after its novice, so a learner who wants the mechanics
finds the [[-expert]] entry next to the tutorial they would
otherwise pick.
The novice variant keeps the unsuffixed id, so it is the default a
beginner reaches for.
The analysis tutorial has no novice variant: its audience is the
teacher or researcher end of the progression.

<<typer commands>>=
_builtin_tutorials_dir = resources.files("learnlog") / "tutorials"

pytorial.add_typer_subcommand(
    app,
    name="tutorial",
    namespace="learnlog",
    tutorial_paths=(
        str(_builtin_tutorials_dir / "getting-started.md"),
        str(_builtin_tutorials_dir / "getting-started-expert.md"),
        str(_builtin_tutorials_dir / "playback-and-tagging.md"),
        str(_builtin_tutorials_dir / "playback-and-tagging-expert.md"),
        str(_builtin_tutorials_dir / "playing-lectures.md"),
        str(_builtin_tutorials_dir / "playing-lectures-expert.md"),
        str(_builtin_tutorials_dir / "export-and-share.md"),
        str(_builtin_tutorials_dir / "export-and-share-expert.md"),
        str(_builtin_tutorials_dir / "metrics.md"),
        str(_builtin_tutorials_dir / "metrics-expert.md"),
        str(_builtin_tutorials_dir / "analysing-progsnap2.md"),
    ),
)
@

The dependency floor, not an import guard, is what makes this work on
Windows.
Pytorial defers its POSIX terminal imports ([[fcntl]], [[pty]],
[[termios]], [[tty]]) since 0.8.1, so [[import pytorial]] at module
scope no longer takes every CLI command down with it.
An import guard here would instead hide a broken installation by
silently removing the tutorial command.

Importable is not yet helpful, though.
Under 0.8.1 a Windows student could start a tutorial and only learn at
its first shell step that it cannot run there, and nothing in
[[learnlog tutorial list]] warned them beforehand.
Every tutorial we ship has shell steps and a bash [[pre_command]], so
that was every tutorial.
Pytorial 0.9.0 moves the answer to where the student looks first: the
list marks such entries \enquote{needs WSL}, [[run]] refuses before the
first step with a message that names WSL, and the help texts carry the
same note.
That is why the floor is 0.9.0, and why learnlog's own tutorials say
nothing about Windows: pytorial owns that guidance, and a step's text
is only ever shown where the run was allowed to start.

A fresh interpreter with all four POSIX modules blocked stands in for
Windows.
The first case checks that our catalog still loads there, the other two
pin the 0.9.0 behaviour the floor promises.

<<test functions>>=
@pytest.mark.parametrize("command, status, expected", [
    (["tutorial", "list"], 0, "getting-started"),
    (["tutorial", "list"], 0, "needs WSL"),
    (["tutorial", "run", "getting-started"], 1, "WSL"),
])
def test_cli_without_posix_terminal_modules(tmp_path, command, status, expected):
    import learnlog
    probe = (
        "import sys\n"
        "sys.modules.update(dict.fromkeys("
        "['fcntl', 'pty', 'termios', 'tty']))\n"
        "from learnlog.cli import app\n"
        "app()\n"
    )
    env = dict(os.environ)
    env["LEARNLOG_SKIP_AUTOSTART"] = "1"
    env["PYTHONPATH"] = str(pathlib.Path(learnlog.__file__).parent.parent)
    env["XDG_STATE_HOME"] = str(tmp_path / "state")
    env["XDG_DATA_HOME"] = str(tmp_path / "data")
    result = subprocess.run(
        [sys.executable, "-c", probe, *command], cwd=tmp_path, env=env,
        capture_output=True, text=True, encoding="utf-8", errors="replace",
        timeout=30,
    )
    output = result.stdout + result.stderr
    assert result.returncode == status, output
    assert expected in output
@

Like [[play]] in \cref{sec:play}, the interactive execution path
needs a real terminal, so our CLI regression test stays on the
TTY-free [[list]] subcommand.
That is enough to verify the host-side wiring and that the shipped
tutorial resources are visible to pytorial.

The getting-started tutorial should still hand the learner over to a
normal shell step for the first file creation task, so the student opens
[[hello.py]] in a preferred editor from the terminal.
With pytorial's shell hooks available for integrated tutorials, every
shell step after the explicit activation lesson installs
[[eval "$(learnlog activate)"]] as a [[pre_command]].
That keeps the tutorial honest about fresh shells while still making the
project environment available for any extra commands the student tries
before returning from the step.
The run step requires a [[python hello.py]] invocation and a greeting on
stdout.
We express both with [[pytorial]] regex matches rather than plain
[[contains]] strings so that equally valid solutions are not rejected:
the command tolerates [[python3]] and a leading [[./]], and the greeting
tolerates case and punctuation variation around [[hello]] and [[world]].
The final playback step looks for [[learnlog play -b]] (accepting the
long [[--batch]] alternative through a regex), then verifies the
recorded run by matching [[hello.py]], the same lenient greeting, and
the reading view's [[Code changes]] heading, which proves the record
reported what the run changed.
We deliberately drop the [[Script: hello.py]] and
[[stdout: Hello, world!]] line prefixes from the validation: those
strings are the log's storage format rather than anything the student
types, and pinning the tutorial to them would make it fragile to
changes in how a record is stored without strengthening the learning
signal.
[[Code changes]] is on the other side of that line: it is the reading
view's own vocabulary, emitted for every run record whatever its diff
looks like (\cref{sec:record-lines}), so it fails when the teaching
changes rather than when the storage does.
We lock the resulting mixed-mode pattern list in with regression tests
against the generated tutorial resource.

Those tests reach a step by its title rather than by its position.  A
tutorial is a document, and a document grows a step in the middle
sooner or later---adding the [[doctor]] step
(\cref{tut:getting-started}) shifted every index after it---so an
assertion keyed on position fails for an edit that is none of its
business, and reports an integer rather than the thing that changed.
Order still carries meaning, so one test spells the sequence out in
full: that keeps the ordering covered while leaving exactly one place
to update when the walkthrough gains a step.

The split tutorials promise that novice and expert variants share
their contract, so a further test walks the three pairs and asserts
that step titles, required patterns, and pre-commands agree step by
step.
The shared chunks in the tutorial sources make this true by
construction today; the test keeps it true if the chunks are ever
unshared.
The instruction-content assertions, by contrast, are keyed to the
variant that carries the text: the mechanics sentences (fresh shells,
re-activation, the viewer's field labels) live only in the expert
variant, and the tests say so explicitly.

<<test functions>>=
def tutorial_resource(filename):
    """Parse the shipped tutorial resource called ``filename``."""
    from importlib import resources

    from pytorial.catalog import parse_tutorial_text

    tutorial_path = resources.files("learnlog") / "tutorials"
    tutorial_path = tutorial_path / filename
    return parse_tutorial_text(
        tutorial_path.read_text(),
        source_path=tutorial_path,
    )

def tutorial_step(filename, title):
    """Return the step called ``title`` from ``filename``."""
    steps = {
        step.title: step
        for step in tutorial_resource(filename).steps
    }
    assert title in steps, sorted(steps)
    return steps[title]

def getting_started_tutorial():
    """Parse the shipped getting-started tutorial resource."""
    return tutorial_resource("getting-started.md")

def getting_started_step(title):
    """Return the shipped getting-started step called ``title``."""
    return tutorial_step("getting-started.md", title)

def test_getting_started_keeps_its_step_order():
    """The walkthrough sets up, checks, writes, runs, inspects."""
    assert [
        step.title for step in getting_started_tutorial().steps
    ] == [
        "Create a fresh project",
        "Activate the project environment",
        "Confirm that runs are being recorded",
        "Create a small program",
        "Run the program",
        "Inspect the recorded run",
    ]

def test_tutorial_list_lists_builtin_tutorials():
    """`learnlog tutorial list` includes our shipped tutorials."""
    from learnlog.cli import app
    from typer.testing import CliRunner

    result = CliRunner().invoke(app, ["tutorial", "list"])
    out = result.stdout

    assert result.exit_code == 0
    assert "getting-started" in out
    assert "getting-started-expert" in out
    assert "playback-and-tagging" in out
    assert "playback-and-tagging-expert" in out
    assert "playing-lectures" in out
    assert "playing-lectures-expert" in out
    assert "export-and-share" in out
    assert "export-and-share-expert" in out
    assert "metrics" in out
    assert "metrics-expert" in out
    assert "analysing-progsnap2" in out
    assert (
        out.index("getting-started")
        < out.index("getting-started-expert")
        < out.index("playback-and-tagging")
        < out.index("playback-and-tagging-expert")
        < out.index("playing-lectures")
        < out.index("playing-lectures-expert")
        < out.index("export-and-share")
        < out.index("export-and-share-expert")
        < out.index("metrics")
        < out.index("metrics-expert")
        < out.index("analysing-progsnap2")
    )

def test_expert_variants_share_their_novice_contract():
    """Each expert twin keeps its novice's step titles and checks."""
    for base in (
        "getting-started",
        "playback-and-tagging",
        "playing-lectures",
        "export-and-share",
        "metrics",
    ):
        novice = tutorial_resource(f"{base}.md")
        expert = tutorial_resource(f"{base}-expert.md")

        assert [step.title for step in novice.steps] == [
            step.title for step in expert.steps
        ], base
        for novice_step, expert_step in zip(
            novice.steps, expert.steps
        ):
            assert (
                novice_step.required_patterns
                == expert_step.required_patterns
            ), (base, novice_step.title)
            assert (
                novice_step.pre_command == expert_step.pre_command
            ), (base, novice_step.title)

def test_getting_started_uses_shell_step_for_file_creation():
    """File creation stays in the shell, on the project Python."""
    step = getting_started_step("Create a small program")
    expert = tutorial_step(
        "getting-started-expert.md", "Create a small program"
    )

    assert step.kind is None
    assert step.edit_file is None
    assert step.required_patterns == ()
    assert step.pre_command == 'eval "$(learnlog activate)"\n'
    assert step.check_command is None
    assert "nano hello.py" in step.instructions
    assert 'print("Hello, world!")' in step.instructions
    assert "re-activates" not in step.instructions

    assert "vim hello.py" in expert.instructions
    assert "drop to a terminal" in expert.instructions
    assert (
        "re-activates the project environment" in expert.instructions
    )

def test_getting_started_reactivates_and_checks_playback():
    """Later shell steps pre-activate the venv and require playback."""
    run_step = getting_started_step("Run the program")
    play_step = getting_started_step("Inspect the recorded run")

    assert run_step.kind is None
    assert run_step.edit_file is None
    assert run_step.pre_command == 'eval "$(learnlog activate)"\n'
    assert tuple(
        (pattern.pattern, pattern.mode)
        for pattern in run_step.required_patterns
    ) == (
        (r"python3?\s+(?:\./)?hello\.py", "regex"),
        (r"(?i)hello[,!\s]*world", "regex"),
    )
    assert run_step.check_command is None
    assert "python hello.py" in run_step.instructions
    assert "fresh shell" not in run_step.instructions

    expert_run = tutorial_step(
        "getting-started-expert.md", "Run the program"
    )
    assert "fresh shell" in expert_run.instructions
    assert (
        "re-activated the project environment"
        in expert_run.instructions
    )

    assert play_step.pre_command == 'eval "$(learnlog activate)"\n'
    assert tuple(
        (pattern.pattern, pattern.mode)
        for pattern in play_step.required_patterns
    ) == (
        (r"learnlog\s+play\b[^\n]*(?:-b\b|--batch\b)", "regex"),
        ("hello.py", "contains"),
        (r"(?i)hello[,!\s]*world", "regex"),
        ("Code changes", "contains"),
    )
    assert play_step.check_command is None
    assert "learnlog play -b" in play_step.instructions
    assert "hello.py" in play_step.instructions

    expert_play = tutorial_step(
        "getting-started-expert.md", "Inspect the recorded run"
    )
    assert "learnlog play -b --raw" in expert_play.instructions
    assert "Script:" in expert_play.instructions
    assert "diff --git" in expert_play.instructions
@

One step is keyed to learnlog's \emph{output} rather than to what the
student types.  The [[doctor]] step (\cref{tut:getting-started}) is
satisfied only by a transcript containing the anchored report's
\enquote{Runs of this interpreter are recorded in} line
(\cref{sec:autostart-diagnosis}), which is what makes it a check that
this interpreter really records runs instead of a check that the
student typed the command.  That buys a real verification at the price
of a string shared between a tutorial and a report neither of which
knows about the other: reword the report, and the shipped tutorial
stops being passable---silently, and only for students.

So we drive the two against each other.  We build the report for a
wired interpreter, assemble the transcript the step would record, and
let [[pytorial]]'s own matcher rule on it; the unwired report goes
through the same matcher to confirm that the step gates on the wiring
and not merely on the word [[doctor]] appearing somewhere.

<<test functions>>=
def test_doctor_step_matches_the_report_it_checks(tmp_path):
    """The tutorial's doctor step gates on a wired report."""
    from learnlog.cli import autostart_report
    from pytorial.run import transcript_matches_patterns

    def transcript(**resolver_args):
        _, lines = autostart_report(**resolver_args)
        return "$ learnlog doctor\n" + "\n".join(lines) + "\n"

    purelib = tmp_path / "site-packages"
    purelib.mkdir()
    project = tmp_path / "lab1"
    (project / ".learnlog").mkdir(parents=True)
    marker = purelib / "learnlog_project_root.txt"
    marker.write_text(str(project) + "\n")
    step = getting_started_step(
        "Confirm that runs are being recorded"
    )

    assert transcript_matches_patterns(
        transcript(
            marker_path=marker,
            sys_prefix=str(project / ".venv"),
        ),
        step.required_patterns,
    )
    assert not transcript_matches_patterns(
        transcript(
            marker_path=tmp_path / "bare" / "marker.txt",
            sys_prefix=str(tmp_path / "bare"),
        ),
        step.required_patterns,
    )
@

The lecture tutorial's tag step (\cref{tut:playing-lectures}) gates on
a listing of the \emph{bundle's} tags, with the options in either
order.
A [[--tags]] listing of the learner's own log is not the step, and
neither is a listing of the bundle without [[--tags]]:

<<test functions>>=
def test_lecture_tags_step_needs_the_bundle_and_the_flag():
    """The tag step accepts either option order, and only both."""
    from pytorial.run import transcript_matches_patterns

    step = tutorial_step(
        "playing-lectures.md", "Find the lecture's parts"
    )

    def matches(command):
        return transcript_matches_patterns(
            f"$ {command}\n", step.required_patterns,
        )

    assert matches("learnlog list -f lecture.learnlog --tags")
    assert matches("learnlog list --tags --from lecture.learnlog")
    assert matches("learnlog list -f lecture.learnlog --tags loops")
    assert not matches("learnlog list --tags")
    assert not matches("learnlog list -f lecture.learnlog")
@


\subsection{The [[init]] command}
\label{sec:init}

The command itself is a thin wrapper over the setup
pipeline from \cref{sec:setup}.  It anchors the repository
at the chosen directory by constructing a [[LearnlogRepo]]
(whose constructor is idempotent---see \cref{gitrepo}),
then dispatches to the language-specific installer.  The
language argument is typed as [[Language]] so Typer
rejects unknown toolchains before we are ever called.

The optional [[-C]] / [[--dir]] flag mirrors the pattern
used by [[set-remote]] and [[push]] and makes [[init]]
scriptable from a parent directory.  The [[--python]]
option is the [[init]]-specific escape hatch discussed in
\cref{sec:setup-target-python}, and [[--force]] the one
that overrides the home-directory refusal
(\cref{sec:setup-home-guard}).

The guard runs before the [[LearnlogRepo]] constructor, not
after: the constructor is what creates [[.learnlog/]], and a
refusal that leaves the directory behind would have already done
the damage it exists to prevent.

<<typer commands>>=
@app.command(name="init")
def init(
    language: Annotated[
        Language,
        typer.Argument(
            help="Language toolchain to set up.",
        ),
    ],
    directory: Annotated[
        str | None,
        typer.Option(
            "-C", "--dir",
            help=(
                "Project directory to set up. "
                "Defaults to the current directory."
            ),
        ),
    ] = None,
    python: Annotated[
        str | None,
        typer.Option(
            "--python",
            help=(
                "Target Python interpreter (name on "
                "PATH or full path).  Defaults to "
                "python3."
            ),
        ),
    ] = None,
    force: Annotated[
        bool,
        typer.Option(
            "--force",
            help=(
                "Set up the project even when the directory "
                "is your home directory or above it."
            ),
        ),
    ] = False,
):
    """Set up a project to record with learnlog."""
    workdir = (
        pathlib.Path(directory).resolve()
        if directory
        else pathlib.Path.cwd()
    )
    if not force:
        refuse_home_root(
            workdir,
            "Change to your project directory, or pass "
            "--force if you really mean this one.",
        )
    LearnlogRepo(workdir)
    if language is Language.python:
        init_python(workdir, python)
@

The end-to-end tests go through Typer's [[CliRunner]] and
confirm the two observable effects: the [[.learnlog/]]
directory appears at the chosen root, and an unknown
language is rejected by Typer before our code runs.  The
Python pipeline is stubbed so the CLI tests do not touch
the network or create real virtual environments.

<<test functions>>=
