\subsection{The [[doctor]] command}
\label{sec:doctor-command}

[[doctor]] prints the report of \cref{sec:autostart-diagnosis}
and, given [[--repair]], acts on it.

The exit status carries the finding too, so the report is usable
from a script or a lab checklist: dead wiring exits non-zero,
the other two states exit zero.  \enquote{Not wired} is
deliberately not a failure---an interpreter nobody prepared is
behaving exactly as intended---whereas dead wiring is the one
state in which a student believes their runs are being recorded
and they are not.

<<typer commands>>=
@app.command(name="doctor")
def doctor(
    repair: Annotated[
        bool,
        typer.Option(
            "--repair",
            help=(
                "Re-wire this interpreter for the learnlog "
                "project in the current directory."
            ),
        ),
    ] = False,
):
    """Check whether this interpreter records runs."""
    <<report the wiring and stop unless repairing>>
    <<choose the project to re-wire for>>
    <<refuse an interpreter we must not wire>>
    <<re-wire and report the result>>
@

The report is printed first whether or not [[--repair]] was
given, so that a repair run leaves behind a record of what it
found as well as what it did.  Without [[--repair]] the finding
\emph{is} the whole command, so it becomes the exit status and we
stop.

<<report the wiring and stop unless repairing>>=
state, lines = autostart_report()
for line in lines:
    typer.echo(line)
if not repair:
    raise typer.Exit(
        code=1 if state is AutostartState.dead else 0
    )
@

Repair has to settle two questions---which project to wire to,
and which interpreter to wire---and it refuses to guess at
either.

The project is whatever the ordinary upward search finds.  When
there is none, the answer is [[init]] rather than a repository
invented somewhere plausible: guessing a root from the current
directory is precisely the behaviour that was removed from the
run path for creating whole-home repositories, and re-introducing
it inside a command called \enquote{repair} would be worse than
leaving it where it was.

That search is already bounded away from the home directory, so a
student standing in [[$HOME]] is told \enquote{no project found}
even when [[$HOME/.learnlog]] is sitting right there, forced into
existence by an earlier [[init --force]]
(\cref{sec:setup-home-guard}).  Saying that would be actively
misleading, so the guard answers first and in its own words---and
its hint is the only honest one available, since a project we
refuse to search for is also one we refuse to wire.

<<choose the project to re-wire for>>=
workdir = find_learnlog_dir()
if workdir is None:
    cwd = pathlib.Path.cwd()
    refuse_home_root(
        cwd,
        "Re-run 'learnlog init python --force' there if "
        "that is really the project you want.",
    )
    raise SystemExit(
        f"No .learnlog/ directory found from {cwd}.  Run "
        f"'learnlog init python' in your project first."
    )
@

The interpreter is this one---the process running
[[doctor]]---because that is the process the report just
described, and repairing a different environment would leave the
finding standing.  Two ways of invoking [[doctor]] make
\enquote{this one} the wrong answer, and both are refused rather
than merely warned about, because the consequence is invisible
from where the student stands.

Outside a virtual environment there is no learnlog wiring of ours
to repair and [[site-packages]] belongs to the system package
manager; [[sys.prefix == sys.base_prefix]] is CPython's own test
for that situation.  Inside a virtual environment that is not the
project's own [[.venv/]]---the shape a [[pipx]] install produces,
where [[doctor]] runs from learnlog's private environment---the
marker would wire the tool rather than the student's programs.
The log would then fill with [[learnlog play]] and [[learnlog list]]
runs, the two the veto lets through (\cref{sec:veto}), and with
nothing the student ever wrote: a record of reading a history that
was never written.  A project that has a [[.venv/]] we are not running from is
exactly that case, and the fix is to activate it first.

The check is conditioned on the project actually having a
[[.venv/]], because installing into an already-active environment
elsewhere is a supported layout (\cref{sec:setup-pipeline}); when
the project has no venv of its own there is nothing to be
mismatched with.

<<refuse an interpreter we must not wire>>=
venv_dir = pathlib.Path(sys.prefix)
if sys.prefix == sys.base_prefix:
    raise SystemExit(
        f"{venv_dir} is not a virtual environment, so there "
        f"is no learnlog wiring here to repair.  Activate "
        f"the project's .venv/ and try again."
    )
project_venv = workdir / ".venv"
if (
    project_venv.is_dir()
    and venv_dir.resolve() != project_venv.resolve()
):
    raise SystemExit(
        f"This command is running from {venv_dir}, not the "
        f"project's {project_venv}.  Activate the project "
        f"venv and run 'learnlog doctor --repair' again."
    )
@

The repair itself is [[write_autostart_files]], the same helper
[[init]] uses (\cref{sec:setup-autostart-hook}); a second way of
writing the wiring would be a second thing to keep in step with
the resolver.

That helper turns filesystem failures into a warning instead of
an abort, which is the right trade for [[init]]---a missing hook
is a lost feature, not a broken project---but it does mean the
call cannot tell us whether it worked.  So we ask the report
again afterwards and let the second answer, rather than the
write, decide both what the command claims and what it exits
with.

<<re-wire and report the result>>=
write_autostart_files(venv_dir, workdir)
typer.echo("")
typer.echo(f"Re-wired this interpreter for {workdir}:")
state, lines = autostart_report()
for line in lines:
    typer.echo(line)
raise typer.Exit(
    code=0 if state is AutostartState.anchored else 1
)
@

Let's verify the three reports and the exit statuses that go with
them.  Each test substitutes a scratch [[site-packages]] for the
interpreter's own and moves [[sys.prefix]] somewhere harmless, so
that the outcome depends only on the fixture and never on how the
