\subsection{The [[list]] command}
\label{sec:list-command}

The [[list]] command gives a compact overview of the development
log, one line per commit---like [[git log --oneline]] but showing
the start time and command instead of the commit message subject.
This is useful for quickly finding a session to inspect with
[[play]], or for checking what a student has been working on.

The lines are grouped under one heading per day.
A listing that is one flat column of timestamps makes the reader do
the grouping in their head, and the grouping is the thing they came
for: a student looking for \enquote{the evening I got the loop
working} navigates by day, not by hash.
A heading per day and a time per entry say the same thing in less
ink, because the date stops being repeated on every line.
The heading spells the weekday out, since [[2026-09-16]] does not
tell a student whether that was a lab day.
It is spelled in English because the rest of this command is:
[[%A]] names the day in the C locale, which is where Python leaves
[[LC_TIME]] unless something calls [[setlocale]], and nothing here
does.
Should the viewer ever need a [[setlocale]] for wide characters, it
has to be scoped to [[LC_CTYPE]], or this listing quietly changes
language.

The same vocabulary that narrows a report (\cref{sec:metrics-command})
narrows the listing: [[--period]] for a rolling window, [[--since]]
and [[--until]] for an explicit one, with a tag name, a date or a
datetime as the boundary.
The default is [[all]], because a listing that silently showed only
this week would be read as a log that had lost the rest.

For symmetry with [[play]] and [[analyse]], the source is passed
with [[--from]] / [[-f]].  This keeps the positional interface free
for command-specific arguments and makes the source selection rule the
same across the commands that can read directories, bundles, or
ProgSnap2 exports.

When a commit contains multiple runs (because the student ran the
same code several times without editing), we show the first run's
details and append [[(N runs)]] so the teacher knows there is more
to explore.
A tagged run shows its names in a trailing [[(tag: lab1)]] marker,
the spelling [[git log --oneline]] made familiar: a tag is a
boundary every report reads (\cref{sec:metrics-tags}), so the
listing must show where the milestones sit.

The [[--success]] and [[--failure]] flags narrow the listing to
commits that contain at least one run of the requested outcome.
A teacher chasing down where a student got stuck passes
[[--failure]]; a student searching for a working version to
revert to passes [[--success]] and hands the last line's hash
to [[learnlog checkout]] (\cref{sec:checkout}).  Because a
commit's outcomes form a set
(\cref{sec:commit-outcomes}), a commit with both a successful
and a failed run appears under both flags---this matters for the
recovery workflow, where the student's most recent known-good
commit must not be hidden just because they later re-ran the
code and broke it.

The [[--runs]] and [[--changes]] options narrow the listing by file.
Three examples show the difference:
[[--runs main.py]] lists every run of a [[main.py]], in whatever
folder;
[[--runs lecture21/main.py]] lists only the runs of the one in
[[lecture21]];
and [[--changes lecture21/main.py]] lists the runs that changed it,
which is where the student edited it (or copied it in with
[[checkout --from]]) rather than where they ran it.
A student looking for \enquote{the version of my lab program that
worked} wants [[--runs]].
A teacher asking \enquote{when did this file take shape} wants
[[--changes]].
Both options take the same patterns, whose rules are given below,
where the filter is applied.
Repeating one option means any of its patterns.
Giving both means a run must match both.
Every other filter of the listing narrows what the ones before it
left, [[--success]] beside [[--since]] for one, and a reader who
combines filters expects them to narrow.

The [[--tags]] flag narrows the listing to the tagged records, and a
regular expression after it narrows those to the tags it matches.
Three examples again:
[[learnlog list --tags]] lists the student's milestones;
[[learnlog list --tags lecture]] lists only the lecture tags among
them, [[lecture21]] and [[lecture22]] alike;
and [[learnlog list -f lecture21.learnlog --tags]] lists the parts a
lecturer marked in a lecture's bundle.
The third is the one the flag exists for.
A student who was given a bundle needs its tag names to hand them to
[[play]] or [[checkout --from]], and the listing reads bundles
already.
So does its [[(tag: ...)]] marker, which shows the run each tag sits
on.

The regular expression is a search, not a full match: [[lecture]]
finds [[lecture21]], and [[^lab1$]] is how to ask for exactly one tag.
This differs from [[analyse]], whose tag boundaries must match the
whole name (\cref{sec:analyse-tags}).
A boundary has to be one moment, and [[lab1]] must not stop at
[[lab10]].
A filter is a question about a set of tags, and a prefix is the
common case.
The match is case-sensitive, like the file patterns, because Git
compares tag names case-sensitively.

The expression is a positional argument, not the value of
[[--tags]].
Typer gives no option whose value may be left out: the bare
[[--tags]] would be refused, and [[--tags --all]] would take
[[--all]] as the expression.
As a flag plus a positional, all of [[--tags]], [[--tags lecture]] and
[[--tags --all]] work, and the positional slot is the one [[--from]]
was introduced to keep free.
An expression without [[--tags]] is refused rather than guessed at,
since the listing has no other positional argument it could mean.

An obvious alternative was a [[-f]] option on [[learnlog tag]], so
that [[learnlog tag -l -f lecture.learnlog]] would list a bundle's
tags.
It collides with Git.
[[learnlog tag]] forwards its arguments to [[git tag]]
(\cref{sec:tag}), where [[-f]] already means \emph{force}, moving an
existing tag.
[[git tag -l]] would also print bare names, without the run each name
marks.

<<typer commands>>=
