Each commit's message body comes out of the scan's message map
rather than from a fresh [[git log -1]] call.
The scan read every message on the branch anyway to build the
trailer map (\cref{sec:run-index}), and a Git call costs a fork
and an exec whatever it asks for, so asking again per commit made
the listing's cost grow with the length of the log.
A hash the map does not know yields an empty body, which
[[parse_run_summary]] turns into [[None]] below---the same
silent skip that a failed per-commit lookup produced before.
We merge in the trailers of the runs the commit records
(\cref{sec:run-join}), filter with the predicate [[hidden_runs]]
picks for this commit (\cref{sec:filter-learnlog})---the tag names
are read first because they are part of that choice---drop commits
that do not match the outcome filter, and then extract the summary
with [[parse_run_summary]].
A commit whose runs are all hidden is already out of [[records]]; the
filter here removes the hidden blocks from a mixed commit, and the
[[None]] test covers the initial commit, which has no run block.

Merging matters here beyond cosmetics: the outcome filters read the
exit code, which lives in the trailer.
Without the merge, [[--success]] and [[--failure]] would match
nothing at all, since every commit would look like an interrupted
run.

