A pattern is read the way a [[.gitignore]] line is, which is the
file-pattern language closest to hand for anyone who has met Git.
A bare name, [[main.py]], matches a file of that name in any folder.
It matches a folder of that name too, and so everything in it:
[[--changes lecture21]] is \enquote{anything in [[lecture21]]}.
A pattern with a slash in it, [[lecture21/main.py]], is anchored at
the project root.
A leading [[./]] (or [[/]], as in [[.gitignore]]) anchors a bare
name, so [[./main.py]] is only the one in the root.

Anchored means relative to the root, not to where the command is
typed.
[[learnlog list]] reads bundles and clones as often as the student's
project, and a bundle has no current directory to be relative to.
The rule is also what [[.gitignore]] does.

Matching goes one path component at a time, with
[[fnmatch.fnmatchcase]] on each.
So [[*]] and [[?]] work but never cross a slash: [[lab1/*.py]] is
the programs in [[lab1]], not in its subfolders.
Matching a whole path with [[fnmatch]] would let [[*]] cross folders.
The case-sensitive variant is used because Git's paths are
case-sensitive.
[[fnmatch.fnmatch]] would fold case on Windows only, and a listing
would then differ between the student's machine and the teacher's.
Backslashes are read as separators, so a pattern typed in PowerShell
means the same.

<<helper functions>>=
def path_matches(pattern, path):
    """Whether root-relative POSIX `path` matches `pattern`.

    A pattern without a slash matches any component of the path;
    one with a slash, or starting with `./` or `/`, matches from
    the root.  Components are matched with `fnmatch`, so `*`
    never crosses a slash.
    """
    pattern = pattern.replace("\\", "/")
    anchored = pattern.startswith(("/", "./"))
    parts = [
        part for part in pattern.split("/") if part not in ("", ".")
    ]
    if not parts:
        return False
    names = path.split("/")
    if len(parts) == 1 and not anchored:
        return any(
            fnmatch.fnmatchcase(name, parts[0]) for name in names
        )
    return len(names) >= len(parts) and all(
        fnmatch.fnmatchcase(name, part)
        for name, part in zip(names, parts)
    )


def matches_any(patterns, paths):
    """Whether any of `paths` matches any of `patterns`."""
    return any(
        path_matches(pattern, path)
        for pattern in patterns for path in paths
    )
@

Let's verify the rules one contrast at a time.
A bare name matches at any depth, and so does a folder name.
Anchoring by a slash or a leading [[./]] restricts to the root.
A glob stays within its component.
A backslash is a separator.
Case matters:

<<test functions>>=
def test_path_matches_bare_names_anywhere():
    from learnlog.cli import path_matches
    assert path_matches("main.py", "main.py")
    assert path_matches("main.py", "lab1/main.py")
    assert path_matches("lab1", "lab1/main.py")
    assert path_matches("lab1/", "x/lab1/main.py")
    assert not path_matches("main.py", "main.pyc")

def test_path_matches_anchored_patterns():
    from learnlog.cli import path_matches
    assert path_matches("lab1/main.py", "lab1/main.py")
    assert not path_matches("lab1/main.py", "x/lab1/main.py")
    assert path_matches("lab1/sub", "lab1/sub/main.py")
    assert path_matches("./main.py", "main.py")
    assert path_matches("/main.py", "main.py")
    assert not path_matches("./main.py", "lab1/main.py")
    assert not path_matches("./", "main.py")

def test_path_matches_globs_stay_in_a_component():
    from learnlog.cli import path_matches
    assert path_matches("*.py", "lab1/sub/main.py")
    assert path_matches("lab1/*.py", "lab1/main.py")
    assert not path_matches("lab1/*.py", "lab2/main.py")
    assert path_matches("lab?/main.py", "lab2/main.py")

def test_path_matches_backslashes_and_case():
    from learnlog.cli import path_matches
    assert path_matches("lab1\\main.py", "lab1/main.py")
    assert not path_matches("Main.py", "main.py")
