\subsection{The [[clone]] command}
\label{sec:clone}

The [[push]] command delivers a local log to a remote repository, but
we also need the reverse: retrieving a remote log locally.
A teacher cloning a student's pushed learnlog to review it, or a
student moving to a new machine, both need a way to get the
[[.learnlog/]] data from the remote.

The command takes a remote URL, performs a standard [[git clone]] into
a staging directory, moves the resulting [[.git/]] directory to
[[.learnlog/]] in the target working directory, and checks the log's
files out there.
We use a staging directory rather than [[git clone --bare]] because
a standard clone produces a properly configured repository with remote
tracking branches---so subsequent [[push]] and [[pull]]
(\cref{sec:pull}) operations work without additional setup.

Like [[push]], we use [[run_git]] with inherited I/O so the
user sees clone progress and can respond to credential prompts.
We deliberately avoid constructing a [[LearnlogRepo]] before the
clone completes, because its constructor auto-initialises an empty
repository when [[.learnlog/]] is absent.
Once the repository \emph{is} in place, constructing one is the point:
that is what configures it.

The guard that refuses to clone over an existing [[.learnlog/]] is
worth a note, because for most of this command's life it was
unreachable and its refusal was the only thing it ever did.
Cloning into an empty directory is the whole point of [[clone]]---a
teacher fetching a student's pushed log, a student moving machines---
and every invocation used to be a recorded run, so [[initialize]]
had already created the very directory the guard then found
(\cref{sec:veto}).
The command could report nothing but the state it had itself just
produced.
Now that an administrative subcommand leaves no trace, an empty
directory is still empty when the guard looks, and the check finally
means what it says: it fires only when the user really is about to
clone over a log that is already there.

<<typer commands>>=
@app.command()
def clone(
    url: Annotated[
        str,
        typer.Argument(
            help="URL of the remote repository to clone.",
        ),
    ],
    directory: Annotated[
        pathlib.Path | None,
        typer.Option(
            "--dir", "-C",
            help=(
                "Target directory for the clone. "
                "Defaults to current directory."
            ),
        ),
    ] = None,
):
    """Clone a remote learnlog repository into .learnlog/."""
    workdir = pathlib.Path(
        directory or os.getcwd()
    ).resolve()
    learnlog_dir = workdir / ".learnlog"

    <<refuse a target we would have to clone over>>
    <<clone into the target and move the repository into place>>
    <<check the cloned files out into the work tree>>

    typer.echo(f"Cloned into {learnlog_dir}")
@

The target has to be empty, and not merely free of a [[.learnlog/]],
because the clone brings files with it.
A log holds the student's source as it was, and the last thing a clone
may do is write those versions over a directory where somebody is
working: an empty directory makes \enquote{check the log's files out}
a safe operation with nothing to weigh against.
A student who wants their log next to work they already have can clone
into an empty directory and move the [[.learnlog/]] across, which is a
decision they made rather than one we made for them.

Creating the target is what turns a typed path into a directory, and
it is also where a typed path that is \emph{not} a directory arrives.
Naming an existing file---a mistyped [[-C]], a tab-completion that
stopped one component short---makes [[mkdir]] raise, and
[[exist_ok]] does not cover it: that flag forgives a directory that is
already there, which is precisely what a file is not.
The same goes for a path whose parent is a file, where the missing
component cannot be created at all.
Left alone, both leave the student looking at a [[FileExistsError]]
traceback for what is, from where they sit, the same mistake the
non-empty target above gets a sentence for.
So we translate them into that sentence, and name the path, because
the whole content of the answer is which path the student got wrong.
Dropping the original exception rather than chaining it is part of
that: the operating system's [[File exists]] is the mechanism, and a
chained traceback is the thing we came here to stop printing.

<<refuse a target we would have to clone over>>=
if learnlog_dir.exists():
    raise SystemExit(
        f".learnlog/ already exists in {workdir}"
    )
try:
    workdir.mkdir(parents=True, exist_ok=True)
except (FileExistsError, NotADirectoryError):
    raise SystemExit(
        f"{workdir} exists and is not a directory. Clone "
        f"checks the log's files out into the target, so it "
        f"needs an empty directory."
    ) from None
if any(workdir.iterdir()):
    raise SystemExit(
        f"{workdir} is not empty. Clone checks the log's "
        f"files out into the target, so it needs an empty "
        f"directory."
    )
@

The clone itself goes into a directory inside the target rather than
into the system temporary directory, and that is what makes it
atomic.
The repository has to end up as [[.learnlog/]] inside the target, and
what moves it there is a rename---which is atomic only within one
filesystem.
[[/tmp]] is routinely a different one, where [[shutil.move]] quietly
degrades into copy-then-delete: an interrupted clone then leaves
behind half a repository, which the guard above reads as a log that is
already there and refuses to replace.
A staging directory beside the destination cannot be on another
filesystem, so [[os.rename]] applies, and either the repository is
there in full or it is not there at all.

Cleaning up catches [[BaseException]] so that [[Ctrl-C]] during a slow
clone is included---an interruption is exactly when a partial
repository would be left---and re-raises, because the user asked for
the command to stop, not for it to report success.

<<clone into the target and move the repository into place>>=
staging = pathlib.Path(
    tempfile.mkdtemp(
        prefix=CLONE_STAGING_PREFIX, dir=workdir,
    )
)
try:
    result = run_git(
        ["clone", url, str(staging)],
        cwd=str(workdir),
    )
    if result.returncode != 0:
        raise SystemExit("Clone failed.")
    os.rename(staging / ".git", learnlog_dir)
except BaseException:
    shutil.rmtree(learnlog_dir, ignore_errors=True)
    raise
finally:
    shutil.rmtree(staging, ignore_errors=True)
<<constants>>=
CLONE_STAGING_PREFIX = ".learnlog-clone-"
@

Moving the repository is where the command used to stop, and what it
left was a work tree with nothing in it.
Git had checked the files out into the staging directory we then
deleted, so every file in the log read as deleted, and the first run
in the clone committed that deletion as the student's work.
Checking them out is therefore not a convenience: it is what makes the
cloned log describe the directory it now lives in.

Constructing the [[LearnlogRepo]] before the checkout does the other
half of the same job.
A cloned repository arrives with none of learnlog's configuration, and
the constructor is what supplies it (\cref{sec:ensure-configured})---
including the exclude file, without which this checkout's own
[[.learnlog/]] would be the first thing the next run staged.

A checkout that fails leaves the log itself intact, so it is reported
rather than cleaned up after: the repository is complete, and the
files can be produced from it at any time with
\mintinline{bash}{learnlog git checkout .}.
Like the bundle writer (\cref{sec:export}), it opts out of [[check]]
so the user gets a sentence instead of a traceback.

A log that holds no files is asked about first, because
\mintinline{bash}{git checkout .} on an empty index is an error and
not a no-op---and cloning a remote that nobody has pushed to yet is
the ordinary way to reach one.
That is a clone with nothing to report, not a clone that went wrong.

<<check the cloned files out into the work tree>>=
repo = LearnlogRepo(workdir)
if repo.git("ls-files").stdout.strip():
    checkout = repo.git(
        "checkout", ".", check=False,
        timeout=GIT_TREE_TIMEOUT,
    )
    if checkout.returncode != 0:
        typer.echo(
            f"Cloned, but could not check out the files: "
            f"{checkout.stderr.strip()}",
            err=True,
        )
