\subsection{The [[git]] command}
\label{sec:git}

Sometimes the log has to be inspected or repaired directly---to check
what was recorded before exporting, to search for a particular
session, or to untangle a state none of the ordinary commands
anticipates.
The natural tool for this is Git itself, but the [[.learnlog/]]
directory requires setting [[GIT_DIR]] and [[GIT_WORK_TREE]]
correctly, which is an implementation detail nobody should have to
know.

The [[git]] subcommand hides this: [[learnlog git log --oneline]]
works exactly like [[git log --oneline]] would if [[.learnlog/]]
were a normal [[.git/]] directory.

The command itself is hidden from [[learnlog --help]], and the
audience is why.
Every ordinary need this command once served has an ordinary command
of its own---[[list]], [[play]], [[tag]], [[export]],
[[checkout]]---and each of those knows the two-commit protocol it is
reading; raw Git does not.
Going back to an older version is the newest of them:
[[learnlog git checkout <hash>]] still works and still detaches
[[HEAD]], but [[learnlog checkout]] (\cref{sec:checkout}) is the
supported way, and the one that keeps the student on the branch.
A student who discovers a general Git passthrough in the help output
will sooner or later drive the log with advice written for a source
repository, and \cref{sec:git-guards} is the catalogue of how that
ends.
So the escape hatch stays---maintainers and teachers debugging a
broken log genuinely need it---but it is something one is \emph{told}
about when the situation calls for it, and whoever types it has
thereby accepted that they are past the guarantees the ordinary
commands maintain.
Hidden is not disabled: the command works exactly as before for
anyone who knows its name, and the guards below still apply to it.
We do not capture Git's output---standard input, output, and error
inherit from the parent process so that pagers, colours, and
interactive Git commands all work naturally.
We also forward Git's exit code, which makes the command usable in
scripts (\eg [[learnlog git diff --quiet]] to test for uncommitted
changes).
For the same reason as [[push]] (\cref{sec:push}), this command does
not go through [[LearnlogRepo.git]]: capturing the output is exactly
what a passthrough must not do.

Staying outside the wrapper does not mean staying outside its
environment discipline.
The passthrough (and [[learnlog tag]] below, which is the same
mechanism with [[tag]] pre-filled) builds its environment with
[[git_environment]] (\cref{sec:git-environment}), because the
redirecting [[GIT_*]] variables reach it the same way they reach a
run: a pre-commit hook exports [[GIT_INDEX_FILE]], and a
[[learnlog git add]] issued from inside that hook would rewrite the
student's own repository's index with it.
What the allow-list costs here is deliberate: a student who sets
[[GIT_AUTHOR_NAME]] in the environment and commits through the
passthrough gets the configured identity instead---the same answer
every learnlog commit gives since the allow-list was introduced,
which is the consistency a student debugging an identity surprise
needs.

Typer normally parses options like [[--oneline]] as its own flags.
We prevent this with three context settings: [[ignore_unknown_options]]
stops Typer from rejecting flags it does not recognise,
[[allow_extra_args]] collects them in [[ctx.args]], and
[[allow_interspersed_args]] set to [[False]] ensures that everything
after the first positional argument is passed through verbatim.

\paragraph{Two commands the passthrough must not forward as written.}
\label{sec:git-guards}
A passthrough inherits its host's assumptions, and this one inherits a
work tree that is the student's project and a repository that Git
itself does not recognise as one.
Two ordinary Git commands become destructive under those assumptions,
and neither of them looks dangerous to the student typing it.

[[git clean -xdf]] is the command every student is eventually told to
run to get rid of build output.
[[.learnlog/]] survives an ordinary [[git clean]] only because it is
listed in [[info/exclude]], and [[-x]] is precisely the flag that says
\enquote{ignore the exclude rules}.
Git's own safety net does not apply either: it protects a directory
named [[.git]], and this one is not.
So the command deletes the entire log---and the lock file with
it---and exits zero without a word.

[[git reset --hard]] is the command students are told to run to undo a
mess.
Under [[--work-tree=.]] the mess it undoes is their source code:
every uncommitted edit in the project, reverted to whatever the last
run happened to record.
With [[HEAD~n]] it rewinds the log and the source together.

What both need is not a smaller passthrough but a corrected one.
For [[clean]] we add an exclusion, because Git applies exclusions
cumulatively: [[-e .learnlog]] leaves every legitimate use of
[[clean -xdf]] working exactly as the student expects, and only puts
the log out of reach.
For [[reset --hard]] no argument can make the command safe---discarding
uncommitted work \emph{is} what it does---so we refuse it, say what it
would have destroyed, and offer the flag that permits it anyway.
A student who really means it types [[--allow-destructive]]; a student
who has been handed the incantation by a forum post gets a sentence
explaining what they were about to lose.

<<typer commands>>=
@app.command(
    name="git",
    hidden=True,
    context_settings={
        "allow_extra_args": True,
        "allow_interspersed_args": False,
        "ignore_unknown_options": True,
    },
)
def git_command(
    ctx: typer.Context,
    allow_destructive: Annotated[
        bool,
        typer.Option(
            "--allow-destructive",
            help=(
                "Permit git commands that throw away "
                "uncommitted work, such as reset --hard."
            ),
        ),
    ] = False,
):
    """Run a git command on the learnlog repository.

    This is an escape hatch for inspecting or repairing the
    log; the ordinary commands (list, play, tag, export)
    are the supported way to work with it, and raw git can
    leave the log in states they do not expect.

    All arguments are forwarded to git, configured to
    operate on the .learnlog/ directory.  For example,
    ``learnlog git log`` lists the recorded commits.
    ``git clean`` is always given an extra exclusion so it
    cannot delete the log, and ``git reset --hard`` is
    refused unless ``--allow-destructive`` precedes it.
    """
    workdir = find_learnlog_dir()
    if workdir is None:
        raise SystemExit(
            "No .learnlog/ directory found."
        )
    args = guard_passthrough(
        ctx.args, workdir, allow_destructive,
    )

    learnlog_dir = workdir / ".learnlog"
    env = git_environment(learnlog_dir, workdir)

    result = run_git(
        args,
        env=env,
        cwd=str(workdir),
    )
    raise typer.Exit(code=result.returncode)
@

Both rules key off the Git subcommand, which is not simply the first
argument: [[git -C dir clean -xdf]] is a perfectly ordinary
invocation.
The subcommand is the first argument that is neither an option nor the
value of one, so finding it means knowing which of Git's own options
take a value.

<<helper functions>>=
def git_subcommand_index(args):
    """Return the position of the Git subcommand in ``args``.

    Returns ``None`` when the arguments name no subcommand.
    """
    skip = False
    for position, argument in enumerate(args):
        if skip:
            skip = False
            continue
        if argument.startswith("-"):
            skip = argument in GIT_OPTIONS_WITH_VALUES
            continue
        return position
    return None
<<constants>>=
GIT_OPTIONS_WITH_VALUES = (
    "-C", "-c", "--git-dir", "--work-tree",
    "--namespace", "--config-env",
)
@

<<helper functions>>=
def guard_passthrough(args, workdir, allow_destructive):
    """Return the arguments to forward to Git.

    Adds the exclusion that keeps ``git clean`` away from the log,
    and raises ``SystemExit`` on a ``git reset --hard`` that the
    student has not explicitly permitted.
    """
    position = git_subcommand_index(args)
    if position is None:
        return list(args)
    if args[position] == "clean":
        return protect_log_from_clean(args, position)
    if args[position] == "reset" and not allow_destructive:
        if has_hard_flag(args[position + 1:]):
            raise SystemExit(describe_reset_refusal(workdir))
    return list(args)
@

The exclusion has to be inserted immediately after [[clean]] rather
than appended, because everything after a [[--]] is a path and an
option there would be read as one.

Which pattern to insert depends on a flag, and the reason is worth
spelling out because the two look contradictory.
Under [[-x]] Git removes everything untracked except what the exclude
patterns match, so the log is saved by \emph{being} excluded.
Under [[-X]] Git removes exactly the files the exclude patterns match,
so the same pattern would order its deletion; what saves the log there
is the negated pattern [[!.learnlog]], which cancels the
[[info/exclude]] entry and takes the log out of the set [[-X]] acts
on.
One flag needs the log ignored and the other needs it un-ignored, so
we look at which one is present.

<<helper functions>>=
def protect_log_from_clean(args, position):
    """Insert the exclusion that keeps ``git clean`` off the log."""
    options = list(args[position + 1:])
    pattern = (
        CLEAN_UNIGNORE_LOG if clean_removes_ignored_only(options)
        else CLEAN_EXCLUDE_LOG
    )
    return list(args[:position + 1]) + ["-e", pattern] + options
<<constants>>=
CLEAN_EXCLUDE_LOG = ".learnlog"
CLEAN_UNIGNORE_LOG = "!.learnlog"
@

[[-X]] has no long form, so it can only arrive as a short option,
possibly bundled with others ([[-Xdf]]).
Scanning the bundle stops at an [[e]], because the rest of that bundle
is [[--exclude]]'s pattern rather than more flags.

<<helper functions>>=
def clean_removes_ignored_only(options):
    """Return whether a ``git clean`` argument list carries ``-X``."""
    pattern_follows = False
    for argument in options:
        if pattern_follows:
            pattern_follows = False
            continue
        if argument == "--":
            break
        if not argument.startswith("-") or argument.startswith("--"):
            continue
        for offset, letter in enumerate(argument[1:], start=1):
            if letter == "e":
                pattern_follows = offset == len(argument) - 1
                break
            if letter == "X":
                return True
    return False
@

[[--hard]] is looked for only before a [[--]], since after one it is a
file name.

<<helper functions>>=
def has_hard_flag(options):
    """Return whether ``--hard`` appears among ``git reset`` options."""
    for argument in options:
        if argument == "--":
            return False
        if argument == "--hard":
            return True
    return False
@

The refusal names the directory whose contents were at stake, because
the student's mental model of [[learnlog git]] is \enquote{something
about the log}, and the surprise worth removing is that this
particular command was about their source code.

<<helper functions>>=
def describe_reset_refusal(workdir):
    """Explain why a ``git reset --hard`` was not forwarded."""
    return (
        f"Refusing git reset --hard: it would discard every "
        f"uncommitted change under {workdir} and rewind the log to "
        f"match.  Re-run it as 'learnlog git --allow-destructive "
        f"reset --hard ...' if that is really what you want."
    )
