\subsection{The [[tag]] command}
\label{sec:tag}

While [[learnlog git tag lab1]] already works via the
[[git]] passthrough, typing [[learnlog tag lab1]] is more
discoverable---students see it in [[learnlog --help]]
alongside [[play]] and [[export]], which reinforces that
tagging is a first-class operation rather than a Git
implementation detail.

The implementation mirrors the [[git]] passthrough
(\cref{sec:git}): we find the [[.learnlog/]] directory,
set the Git environment, and forward all arguments to
[[git tag]] through [[run_git]] (\cref{sec:starting-git}).

[[git tag]] with no ref tags HEAD, and which commit that is depends
on something outside this command: whether [[learnlog tag]] is itself
recorded.
It is not (\cref{sec:veto}), and that is half of what makes the
shipped workflow work.
While administrative commands still logged themselves, HEAD at this
moment was the header commit of the [[learnlog tag]] run---so the tag
named the act of tagging, on a commit that every view filters out.
The other half is that HEAD is still not always the last thing the
student \emph{ran}: a student who runs their program, types
[[learnlog list]] to look at it, and then tags, has a review run at
the tip (\cref{sec:review-runs}), and a tag on it would name the act
of looking---again a record the views hide by default, and one that
would start the milestone's period at the moment of a listing
(\cref{sec:metrics-tags}).
So the bare [[learnlog tag name]] form aims the tag itself: it walks
back from the tip past learnlog's own records---the review runs and
the plumbing, by the same rule the views hide them
(\cref{sec:filter-learnlog})---to the newest record that is the
student's work, a program run or a checkout, and hands that commit to
[[git tag]] explicitly.
The commit is the run's trailer when the run finished, the header
otherwise, so that the tag sits where HEAD would have been had the
student tagged straight after the run.
Only the bare form is aimed.
An explicit ref means the student knows where they want the tag, and
the option forms ([[-l]], [[-d]], [[-a ... -m ...]]) are Git's own
grammar, which a passthrough should not parse; a log with no student
run at all falls back to HEAD rather than refusing, because a tag on
a review run is still a tag.
The result is that [[learnlog play lab1]] starts where the student
thinks they left off.

That checkpoint is read as a \emph{start marker}, the way
[[git describe]] reads one: the tag names the run it sits on and
every run after it, until the next tag (\cref{sec:metrics-tags}).
So the shipped advice is to tag when \emph{starting} a
milestone---run the first thing of lab~2, then
[[learnlog tag lab2]]---and the wrong habit, tagging when finishing,
silently shifts every per-milestone report by one.
That mistake would otherwise surface weeks later in a report, so a
successful creation answers by naming the run the tag landed on and
restating the rule.
The guard around the confirmation is deliberately narrow---only the
bare [[learnlog tag name]] form, the one the tutorial teaches, earns
the line---because a missed confirmation costs nothing while a wrong
one (after a listing, a deletion, or a tag on an explicit ref) would
mislead.

