The argument is whatever the student has to hand: the seven-character
hash that [[list]] and the viewer print, a tag, or any revision Git
understands.
It is peeled with [[git rev-parse]] before [[resolve_record_commit]]
sees it, in that order for the reason [[play --from]] gives
(\cref{sec:play}): the record map is keyed by full hashes, so a tag or
an abbreviated hash handed over directly would miss it and fall to
the topological guess that \cref{sec:trailer-refs} explains is wrong
whenever two runs overlapped.
Resolving matters because a tag lands on the trailer of the last run,
and a trailer's tree is not that run's own; the header is the state
the run executed.
The commit then has to be on the recording branch: a hash from
elsewhere in the object database is not \enquote{a run in this log},
and refusing it is what keeps the restore inside the history the
student can see.
A typo therefore fails before anything is recorded.

