goes to this project's log, [[repo]].

A merge or rebase the student left half-finished in the log is the one
state the restore cannot be made safe in.
[[begin_run]] does not publish its index while one is in progress
(\cref{sec:beginning-a-run}), so the shared index the restore would
rewrite is stale, and [[read-tree --reset]] discards the unmerged
entries that are the operation's whole state.
Refusing costs the student one [[learnlog git merge --abort]]; going
ahead would cost them the merge.

<<refuse while a Git operation is unfinished>>=
unfinished = repo.unfinished_operation()
if unfinished:
    raise SystemExit(
        f"A Git operation is unfinished in the log ({unfinished}); "
        "finish or abort it, for example with "
        "learnlog git merge --abort, before checking out."
    )
@

The snapshot is the same [[begin_run]] every program run starts with,
named as a [[learnlog]] run so the readers classify it
(\cref{sec:filter-learnlog}), and its header hash is what the
confirmation will hand the student as the way back.
A snapshot that cannot be made ends the command before any file is
touched, because an unrecorded overwrite is the one outcome this
command exists to rule out.

<<record the files about to be replaced>>=
<<the command line to record>>
try:
    repo.begin_run(
        "learnlog", recorded, datetime.datetime.now(),
        include=source_files(recorded, workdir),
    )
except GitError as error:
    raise SystemExit(
        "Could not record your current files, so nothing was "
        f"restored: {error}"
    )
snapshot = repo.run_commit
@

The command line recorded is the shape that ran, options included, so
that the log itself says whether files came from this project, from
another log and into which directory, or from a snapshot being
restored; and a source file among those words is recorded with the
snapshot, as \cref{sec:source-files} explains.

<<the command line to record>>=
if restore:
    recorded = [CHECKOUT_SUBCOMMAND, "--restore", run]
elif source is not None:
    recorded = [CHECKOUT_SUBCOMMAND, "--from", source]
    if output is not None:
        recorded += ["-o", output]
    recorded.append(run)
else:
    recorded = [CHECKOUT_SUBCOMMAND, run]
@

The trailer closes the record whether the restore succeeded or not,
so that a failed checkout reads as one in the log rather than as a
run that never ended.
What it carries differs: a success records the confirmation as the
run's output and names the restored tree
(\cref{sec:restore-code-state}), a failure records Git's complaint
and leaves the tree at its default, since how far a failed
[[read-tree]] got is not something to guess at.
Only [[GitError]] is caught; anything else is not a failed restore
but a failed command, and it propagates with the record left open,
exactly as a killed program run leaves its header.

<<restore the run and close its record>>=
<<the confirmation lines>>
failure = None
try:
    repo.restore_code_state(target)
    tree = f"{target}^{{tree}}"
except GitError as error:
    failure = str(error)
    tree = None
<<close the record with the outcome>>
@

<<the confirmation lines>>=
confirmation = [
    headline,
    f"Your previous files were recorded as {snapshot[:7]}: "
    f"learnlog checkout {snapshot[:7]} or learnlog checkout "
    "--restore brings them back",
]
<<close the record with the outcome>>=
if failure is None:
    io_log = "".join(f"stdout: {line}\n" for line in confirmation)
else:
    io_log = f"stderr: {failure}\n"
repo.finalize_run(
    io_log=io_log,
    exception_info=None,
    end_time=datetime.datetime.now(),
    exit_code=0 if failure is None else 1,
    tree=tree,
)
@

\subsubsection{Copying a run's files in from another log}
\label{sec:checkout-from}

A student who has played back a lecture's recording
([[learnlog play -f lecture.learnlog]], \cref{sec:play}) wants the
code at some point of it in their own project, to run and change.
[[learnlog checkout --from lecture.learnlog <hash>]] copies that
run's files in and records the copy as a checkout in the student's
log, and the record is the point: the [[Run:]] line names the source
and the ref as typed, the confirmation recorded as the run's output
names the full hash, and the trailer's tree is the project with the
copied files in it, so the student's next run diffs against the
lecture's code and a teacher reading the log sees what was taken and
what was done with it.
The bundle itself joins the snapshot ([[source_files]],
\cref{sec:source-files}), as it does for a playback, so a student who
copies without ever playing still leaves the recording in their log.

The files are copied in \emph{over} the project, not in place of it.
An own-log checkout replaces the work tree because its target is an
earlier state of this very project; a lecture's tree is not, and a
student whose lab files vanished because they fetched a lecture's
would have learnt the wrong lesson about a command meant to be safe.
So files of the same name are overwritten---they are in the snapshot
the record starts with---and everything else stays.
The copy goes into a directory named after the source unless
[[--output]] says otherwise, so a student who keeps each lecture in
its own folder gets that for free.

Copying files rather than fetching objects is what lets one code path
serve every kind of source [[resolve_source]] knows, and it keeps the
other log's history out of the student's object store; the trailer's
tree is built from the work tree afterwards ([[stage_work_tree]],
\cref{sec:stage-work-tree}), so it holds exactly what is on disk and
[[git status]] is clean.
[[extract_code_state]] (\cref{sec:extract-code-state}) does the
copying, and its [[tarfile]] filter is the guard against a tree that
would write outside the destination.
The way back is the same as for any checkout: the snapshot taken
first, restored as a replace, removes the copied files again.

<<copy the run's files in and close its record>>=
headline = (
    f"{run} from {source} -> {subject}; files copied into "
    f"{target_dir} ({target}), your next run continues from here"
)
<<the confirmation lines>>
failure = None
try:
    destination.mkdir(parents=True, exist_ok=True)
    extract_code_state(origin, target, destination)
    tree = repo.stage_work_tree()
except (GitError, OSError) as error:
    failure = str(error)
    tree = None
<<close the record with the outcome>>
@

\subsubsection{Undoing a checkout}
\label{sec:checkout-restore}

Every checkout starts by recording the files it is about to replace,
and that snapshot is the checkout's own header commit---the hash
[[list]] prints on the checkout's line and the confirmation names as
the way back.
So \enquote{the files from before that checkout} is always one
[[learnlog checkout <hash>]] away, and [[--restore]] exists to save
the student the hash.

It is a chooser rather than a rule, because after two checkouts
\enquote{my code} is ambiguous: the code before the lecture came in,
or the edits made to the lecture's code since?
And a bare undo cannot chain, since a restore is itself a checkout
and becomes the newest one; a second bare undo would only undo the
undo.
The command therefore lists the log's checkouts, newest first and
numbered, each with the command as recorded and the snapshot it would
bring back, and asks for a number---the most recent by default, which
is the common case.
Restores are listed like any other checkout, so one can be redone.
The number can be given as the argument instead, for a student who
already knows, and when there is no terminal to ask on the default is
taken and said, so a script gets the common case rather than a hang.
A number outside the list is a mistake to report, not to clamp.

<<helper functions>>=
def checkout_records(repo):
    """The log's checkouts, newest first.

    Each is ``(commit, start, command)``: the checkout's header
    commit, which snapshots the files from before it, and the
    start time and command line as `parse_run_summary` reads them.
    """
    scan = scan_run_commits(repo)
    found = []
    for commit in reversed(get_commits(repo)):
        summary = parse_run_summary(
            scan.messages.get(commit["hash"], "")
        )
        if summary is not None and is_checkout_command(summary[0]):
            found.append((commit["hash"], summary[1], summary[0]))
    return found
@

The chosen header is the target, with no ref to peel and no trailer
to step back from: a header commit is a run's record and its tree is
the snapshot.
The record's command line carries the number, so the log says which
checkout was undone.
A failed checkout is listed with the rest: its files were never
touched, but its header is a snapshot like any other and the list is
of snapshots.

<<choose the checkout to undo>>=
checkouts = checkout_records(repo)
if not checkouts:
    raise SystemExit(
        "Nothing to restore: there is no checkout in this log."
    )
typer.echo("Your checkouts, newest first:")
for number, (commit, start, command) in enumerate(checkouts, 1):
    when = (
        UNKNOWN_START if start is None
        else start.strftime("%Y-%m-%d %H:%M")
    )
    typer.echo(
        f"{number:>3}  {when}  {command}"
        f"   files before it: {commit[:7]}"
    )
<<take the number from the argument or the prompt>>
target, _, undone = checkouts[choice - 1]
subject = repo.git(
    "log", "-1", "--format=%s", target, check=False,
).stdout.strip()
run = str(choice)
headline = (
    f"Brought back the files from before {undone} "
    f"({target[:7]}); your next run continues from here"
)
<<constants>>=
UNKNOWN_START = "----------  --:--"
@

<<take the number from the argument or the prompt>>=
if run is not None:
    try:
        choice = int(run)
    except ValueError:
        raise SystemExit(
            f"{run} is not the number of a checkout; "
            f"give 1 to {len(checkouts)}."
        )
else:
    try:
        choice = typer.prompt(
            "Bring back the files from before which one?",
            default=1, type=int,
        )
    except typer.Abort:
        choice = 1
        typer.echo("No answer; taking 1, the most recent.")
if not 1 <= choice <= len(checkouts):
    raise SystemExit(
        f"There is no checkout {choice}; give 1 to {len(checkouts)}."
    )
@


<<confirm the restore and the way back>>=
if failure is not None:
    raise SystemExit(f"Restoring {run} failed: {failure}")
for line in confirmation:
    typer.echo(line)
@

Let's verify the whole promise on one history: two runs, then an
edit that was never run.
The checkout brings the first run's files back and removes the file
the second run added; the branch grows by exactly one record, whose
header holds the unrun edit; the trailer's tree is the restored one,
so the work tree is clean against the tip; and [[HEAD]] is still on
the branch.

<<test functions>>=
def _record_run(repo, source, moment):
    """Write ``source`` to hello.py and record one run of it."""
    (repo.workdir / "hello.py").write_text(source)
    repo.begin_run("hello.py", [], moment)
    header = repo.run_commit
    repo.finalize_run(
        io_log="",
        exception_info=None,
        end_time=moment + datetime.timedelta(seconds=1),
        exit_code=0,
    )
    return header

def test_checkout_restores_and_records(workdir, monkeypatch):
    """Files come back; the replaced ones go into the log first."""
    from learnlog.cli import app
    from typer.testing import CliRunner
    repo = LearnlogRepo(workdir)
    branch = repo.recording_branch()
    monkeypatch.chdir(workdir)
    runner = CliRunner()
    old = _record_run(
        repo, 'print("v1")\n', datetime.datetime(2026, 1, 1, 10, 0),
    )
    (workdir / "extra.py").write_text("pass\n")
    _record_run(
        repo, 'print("v2")\n', datetime.datetime(2026, 1, 1, 10, 5),
    )
    (workdir / "hello.py").write_text('print("v3, never run")\n')
    before = int(repo.git(
        "rev-list", "--count", branch,
    ).stdout.strip())

    result = runner.invoke(app, ["checkout", old[:7]])
    assert result.exit_code == 0, result.output

    assert (workdir / "hello.py").read_text() == 'print("v1")\n'
    assert not (workdir / "extra.py").exists()
    after = int(repo.git(
        "rev-list", "--count", branch,
    ).stdout.strip())
    assert after == before + 2
    header = repo.git("rev-parse", f"{branch}~1").stdout.strip()
    subject = repo.git(
        "log", "-1", "--format=%s", header,
    ).stdout.strip()
    assert subject == f"Run: learnlog checkout {old[:7]}"
    assert repo.git(
        "show", f"{header}:hello.py",
    ).stdout == 'print("v3, never run")\n'
    assert repo.git(
        "rev-parse", f"{branch}^{{tree}}",
    ).stdout.strip() == repo.git(
        "rev-parse", f"{old}^{{tree}}",
    ).stdout.strip()
    assert repo.git("status", "--porcelain").stdout == ""
    assert repo.git(
        "symbolic-ref", "--quiet", "HEAD",
    ).stdout.strip() == branch
    assert f"recorded as {header[:7]}" in result.output
    assert "next run continues" in result.output
@

