The point of restoring onto the branch is the run that follows, so
the last test records one: its header sits on the checkout's trailer,
[[list]] shows the checkout between the two, and the churn that
\cref{metrics} charges the new run is the one line the student added
after the restore, not the reversion.
The listing is read through its entry lines, because the checkout is
dated by the clock while the fixture's runs are not: it sits under
today's heading and the run after it re-opens the fixture's day,
which is the commit-order rule of \cref{sec:list-command} at work.

