\subsection{The [[play]] command}
\label{sec:play}

The most common use of [[play]] is jumping to a tagged
session: [[learnlog play lab1]] starts playback from the
commit tagged [[lab1]].
Therefore the optional positional argument is a Git ref
(tag name, branch, or commit hash), not the source
directory.
The source---a directory containing [[.learnlog/]], a
bundle file, or a ProgSnap2 zip---is specified with
[[--from]] / [[-f]] when needed; it defaults to the
current directory.

The [[-b]] / [[--batch]] flag selects batch mode
(\cref{sec:play-batch}), which prints all commits to standard
output instead of launching the curses viewer.
When the flag is not given explicitly, we auto-detect: if
standard output is not a terminal (for example when piped to
[[less]]), batch mode activates automatically.
This means [[learnlog play | less]] just works without the user
having to remember the flag.

What playback shows by default is the student's own work: their
program runs, and the checkouts that went back to one
(\cref{sec:checkout}).
What it leaves out is learnlog reading itself.
The [[play]], [[list]] and [[metrics]] runs are recorded in full and
reach every researcher path unchanged (\cref{progsnap2},
\cref{metrics}), but a log read ten times in an evening buries the
writing under the reading, and the view a student opens to see their
work should show their work (\cref{sec:filter-learnlog}).
Also left out is learnlog's plumbing---the [[pip]] install that
[[learnlog init]] performs, and whatever an older version recorded of
the administrative subcommands that no longer log themselves at all
(\cref{sec:veto}).
The [[--all]] / [[-a]] flag restores both.
A record the student tagged is never hidden, whatever it holds: the
tag is their milestone marker, and [[learnlog play lab1]] has to be
able to start on it (\cref{sec:visible-commits}).
The commit list is thinned before either medium sees it, so that the
viewer's position counter counts records the student can read and
never lands on a placeholder.

[[--raw]] / [[-r]] is the other axis, and the two compose because they
answer different questions.
[[--all]] decides \emph{which} runs are shown; [[--raw]] decides
\emph{how} a run is shown---as the reading card of \cref{render}, or
as the [[git show]] text the log actually stores.
A student debugging their own record wants
[[learnlog play -b --all --raw]] and gets exactly the union, not a
mode that has to be chosen between.
The reading view is the default because the audience is a first-year
student and the stored format is full of vocabulary that has nothing
to teach them; the raw view stays reachable because the same log is
also the thing a teacher, a bug report, and a [[git]] command have to
talk about.

Colour follows the rule every well-behaved command-line tool follows,
and [[use_color]] (\cref{sec:use-color}) states it: [[--color]] on,
[[--no-color]] off, and by default on only when standard output is a
terminal and [[NO_COLOR]] is unset.
The default is what makes [[learnlog play -b > log.txt]] and
[[learnlog play -b | grep]] produce plain text without the student
knowing there was a decision to make, and it is the same detection
[[--batch]] already does one line below.
Typer's [[CliRunner]] connects no terminal, so a test that invokes
[[play]] through it can only ever exercise the automatic \emph{off}
branch; the automatic \emph{on} branch is covered where it can be, by
[[render]]'s unit test of [[use_color]] against a stub stream.

Without a starting ref the viewer opens on the newest record, not the
oldest.
A student who types [[learnlog play]] has just run something and wants
to see how it went; the beginning of the log is where they were weeks
ago.
The newest record used to need a special case: playback is recorded
(\cref{sec:review-runs}), so its own header commit ended the list and
the viewer had to step back one to land on what the student had just
done.
That commit is now one of the hidden ones and is not in the list at
all, so the last record already is the one that made the student
look.
Under [[--all]] the run in progress is back in the list and the viewer
opens on it: the student asked to see everything, and the present is
where it is.
Batch mode keeps starting from the beginning: it prints rather than
positions, and [[learnlog play -b | less]] is how a reader gets the
whole log.

The third way to choose a position is the one the student's own
history supplies.
Playback at a lecture's tag, half read; [[q]]; a
[[learnlog checkout]] back to an earlier version (\cref{sec:checkout});
an hour of experiments; and then the wish to pick the lecture up where
it stopped, without remembering a hash.
[[--continue]] / [[-c]] opens on the record the previous playback
showed last, which the log already knows: it is the last [[Viewed:]]
line of that playback's trailer, and [[last_playback]]
(\cref{sec:last-viewed}) reads it back.
Nothing is stored for this---the log is the bookmark---and because the
bookmark is a hash rather than a position, the checkouts and runs made
in between, which only append records, do not move it.
The flag composes with the other two on the same terms as they
compose with each other: [[--all]] decides which records are shown,
[[--raw]] how, [[--continue]] where to open.
In batch mode it prints from the bookmark to the end, the half of the
log the student has not read yet.

The bookmark is read from the student's own log, whatever log is being
played.
A playback is recorded where it was run, so a student who plays a
lecture's bundle with [[-f lecture.learnlog]] leaves the trace in
their own project's log, with the lecture's hashes in it; the bundle
itself holds the lecturer's playbacks, which are not the student's
place in anything.
So [[-c]] starts from the log of the current project and asks it for
the newest playback, then plays whatever that playback played: with
[[-f]] given, only playbacks of that same source count, and without
it the last playback wins and brings its own source along---
[[learnlog play -c]] after [[learnlog play -f lecture.learnlog]] is
the lecture again, and a line on standard error says so, where it
cannot end up in a batch listing.
A project with no log of its own has nothing to continue, even when
the log it is about to play holds playbacks: those are somebody
else's.

A starting ref is looked up in the thinned list, and a ref that names
a record the view hides is refused with the flag that would show it,
rather than silently moved to the nearest visible record:
[[learnlog play <hash>]] opening somewhere other than [[<hash>]] is
exactly the quiet disagreement the ref resolution of
\cref{sec:run-join} exists to avoid.

<<helper functions>>=
def record_index(records, commits, matches, missing):
    """Index in ``records`` of the commit ``matches`` accepts.

    ``records`` is the list a reading view shows and ``commits``
    the full log.  Exits with ``missing`` when nothing matches,
    and with a message naming ``--all`` when the match is a
    record the view hides.
    """
    for index, commit in enumerate(records):
        if matches(commit):
            return index
    for commit in commits:
        if matches(commit):
            raise SystemExit(
                f"Record {commit['hash'][:7]} is one of "
                "learnlog's own runs, which play hides.  "
                "Use --all to start playback there."
            )
    raise SystemExit(missing)
@

Let's verify the three answers: an index into the shown list, the
