@

The period grammar has one rule beyond what \cref{sec:metrics-periods}
defines: [[--period]] and [[--since]]/[[--until]] cannot be combined.
A report whose header could describe either of two windows is worse
than a refusal, so the resolver stops before reading anything.
Each command has its own default window ([[week]] for the four
single-family reports, [[all]] for [[trends]]), which is why the
option itself defaults to nothing and the command supplies the
fallback.

A boundary tries the log's tag names before the calendar, so a tag
named like a date shadows the date deliberately rather than
vanishing silently (\cref{sec:metrics-tags}).
The date/tag asymmetry in [[--until]] is intentional: both mean
\enquote{up to but not including the next thing}---for a date the
next day, for a tag its own first run.

<<imports>>=
import datetime
import json
<<helper functions>>=
def metrics_inputs(source, period, by, since, until, idle,
                   default_period=Period.WEEK):
    """Resolve the source, window, sessions and buckets.

    Returns a `MetricsInputs`; the caller must call its
    `cleanup()` when done, because the source may be a temporary
    checkout and a series needs a directory for its snapshots.
    """
    refuse_two_windows(period, since, until)
    repo, tmpdir = resolve_source(source)
    try:
        <<resolve the metrics window and buckets>>
    except BaseException:
        if tmpdir is not None:
            shutil.rmtree(tmpdir, ignore_errors=True)
        raise
    return MetricsInputs(repo, tmpdir, buckets, idle_delta, header, by)
@

The header names the choices that move the numbers---window, idle
threshold, and how many runs never finalised---so that two reports can
be compared only when they were made the same way.

Tag periods change three lines of this resolution and no more.
The boundaries are read only when something will consume them---a
[[--by tag]] series, or a [[--since]]/[[--until]] that may name
one.
A [[--by tag]] series with no explicit window defaults to [[all]]
rather than the week the other families default to: a milestone
series is inherently a whole-history question, and a seven-day
window would collapse a semester's labs into one or two rows led by
a tag from far outside the window.
The header shows the resolved window either way, so the differing
default is visible in every report, and an explicit [[--period]]
still wins.

<<resolve the metrics window and buckets>>=
records = run_records(repo)
idle_delta = datetime.timedelta(minutes=idle)
boundaries = (
    tag_boundaries(repo)
    if by is Granularity.TAG or since or until else []
)
starts = tag_starts(boundaries)
if since or until:
    try:
        window = explicit_window(
            since, until, records, starts=starts,
        )
    except ValueError as error:
        raise SystemExit(str(error)) from error
elif by is Granularity.TAG and period is None:
    window = period_window(Period.ALL, records)
else:
    window = period_window(period or default_period, records)
in_window = filter_window(records, *window)
if by is Granularity.TAG:
    buckets = tag_buckets(
        in_window, boundaries, window, idle_delta,
    )
else:
    sessions = segment_sessions(in_window, idle_delta)
    buckets = bucket_sessions(sessions, by, window)
header = [
    f"window: {window_header(window, since, until, starts)}, "
    f"{len(in_window)} runs",
    f"idle threshold: {idle} min between runs starts a new session",
]
if by is Granularity.TAG:
    header.append(TAG_BUCKET_NOTE)
incomplete = sum(run.end is None for run in in_window)
if incomplete:
    header.append(
        f"incomplete runs (no end time, counted as 0 s): {incomplete}"
    )
