Which linter runs is decided in three steps, the first that speaks
winning: the [[--linter]] option, the [[learnlog.linter]] setting in
the project's [[.learnlog]] repository, and the default, [[ruff]]
(\cref{sec:metrics-lint}).
The option is for the one-off second opinion; the setting is for a
course that has settled on pylint's deeper checks and does not want
every student to remember an option.
The setting is Git configuration rather than a file of our own
because the [[.learnlog]] repository already carries the project's
settings---identity, exclusions---and [[git config]] brings locking
and parsing with it.
Being configuration it is local to the checkout: neither [[export]]
nor [[clone]] carries it, so a report on a bundle runs the default.
The header always names the tool, its path, and which of the three
steps chose it, so that two students' reports can be told apart when
they ran different tools.

<<constants>>=
METRICS_LINTER_OPTION = typer.Option(
    "--linter",
    help=(
        "Linter to run: ruff (the default) or pylint. Overrides the "
        "learnlog.linter setting (see: learnlog config linter)."
    ),
)
<<metrics linter option>>=
linter: Annotated[Linter | None, METRICS_LINTER_OPTION] = None,
<<helper functions>>=
def configured_linter(repo):
    """The `learnlog.linter` setting of `repo`, or `None`.

    A value that names no linter is reported on stderr and ignored,
    so a typo in the setting costs a warning, not the report.
    """
    result = repo.git("config", "--get", LINTER_CONFIG_KEY, check=False)
    if result.returncode != 0:
        return None
    value = result.stdout.strip()
    try:
        return Linter(value)
    except ValueError:
        typer.echo(
            f"warning: {LINTER_CONFIG_KEY} is {value!r}, which names "
            f"no linter; using {DEFAULT_LINTER.value}",
            err=True,
        )
        return None
<<helper functions>>=
def choose_linter(repo, requested):
    """Resolve the linter to run and describe the choice.

    Returns `(linter, note)`: `linter` is what `find_linter` found
    (or `None`), `note` the header line naming the tool, its path,
    and whether `--linter`, the `learnlog.linter` setting, or the
    default chose it.
    """
    if requested is not None:
        tool, origin = requested, "--linter"
    else:
        tool = configured_linter(repo)
        origin = LINTER_CONFIG_KEY if tool else "default"
    linter = find_linter(repo.workdir, tool)
    if linter is None:
        note = (
            f"{tool.value} not found ({origin}): pip install {tool.value}"
            if tool else LINT_INSTALL_HINT
        )
    else:
        note = f"linter: {linter[0]} ({linter[1]}; {origin})"
    return linter, note
@

The setting is read and written with [[learnlog config]], which knows
its keys---only [[linter]] so far---and validates the value before
Git stores it, so that what is stored is always something
[[configured_linter]] accepts.
Like every subcommand outside [[REVIEW_SUBCOMMANDS]]---[[checkout]]
excepted, which records itself (\cref{sec:checkout})---it leaves no
run record.

<<helper functions>>=
class ConfigKey(str, enum.Enum):
    """The project settings `learnlog config` manages."""

    LINTER = "linter"
<<typer commands>>=
@app.command(name="config")
def config_setting(
    key: Annotated[
        ConfigKey,
        typer.Argument(help="Setting to show or change: linter."),
    ],
    value: Annotated[
        str | None,
        typer.Argument(help="New value; omit to show the current one."),
    ] = None,
    unset: Annotated[
        bool,
        typer.Option("--unset", help="Remove the setting."),
    ] = False,
    directory: Annotated[
        pathlib.Path | None,
        typer.Option(
            "--dir", "-C",
            help="Directory containing .learnlog/.",
        ),
    ] = None,
):
    """Show or change a project setting kept in .learnlog/."""
    repo = LearnlogRepo(resolve_workdir(directory))
    git_key = f"learnlog.{key.value}"
    if unset:
        repo.git("config", "--unset", git_key, check=False)
        return
    if value is None:
        current = repo.git("config", "--get", git_key, check=False)
        if current.returncode == 0:
            typer.echo(current.stdout.strip())
        else:
            typer.echo(
                f"{key.value} is not set; default: {DEFAULT_LINTER.value}"
            )
        return
    if key is ConfigKey.LINTER and value not in [t.value for t in Linter]:
        raise typer.BadParameter(
            "linter must be one of: "
            + ", ".join(t.value for t in Linter)
        )
    repo.git("config", git_key, value)
